{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-929780f8fa587fd7", "type": "tool_result", "content": "0\tKVAE: Family of Tokenizers for Multimodal\n1\tGenerative Models\n2\t\n3\tarXiv:2608.05798v1 [cs.CV] 6 Aug 2026\n4\t\n5\tAndrey Shutkin1\n6\tKandinsky Lab\n7\t\n8\tDenis Parkhomenko1, 2, 3, 4\n9\tKandinsky Lab\n10\t\n11\tKirill Malakhov2, 4\n12\tKandinsky Lab\n13\t\n14\tIlia Vasiliev2, 4\n15\tKandinsky Lab\n16\t\n17\tDavid Chikovani2, 4\n18\tKandinsky Lab\n19\t\n20\tAlexander Ivanov3\n21\tKandinsky Lab\n22\t\n23\tIvan Kirillov3\n24\tKandinsky Lab\n25\tIlia Trushkin2\n26\tKandinsky Lab\n27\t\n28\tIvan Mikheev1\n29\tKandinsky Lab\n30\t\n31\tAzat Saginbaev3\n32\tKandinsky Lab\n33\t\n34\tKirill Chernyshev1\n35\tKandinsky Lab\n36\tValeriya Kobenko2\n37\tKandinsky Lab\n38\tEgor Silvestrov3\n39\tKandinsky Lab\n40\t\n41\tKonstantin Zakharov2\n42\tKandinsky Lab\n43\t\n44\tAbstract\n45\tLatent diffusion modeling (LDM), a prominent paradigm, utilizes tokenizers to\n46\tmap input signal to compressed representation. This dependency positions tokenizer as an integral part of generation process itself, since it affects learning\n47\tspeed, quality of synthesized samples and lay foundation for later applications.\n48\tThis report presents series of KVAE tokenizers for audio, image and video, all\n49\tdesigned for subsequent text-conditioned generation: KVAE-Audio, a continuous\n50\tfull-band 48 kHz tokenizer with a 50 Hz latent of 64 channels; KVAE-3D – two\n51\tcausal video tokenizers for 4x16x16 and 4x8x8 compression; KVAE-2D, an image\n52\tmodel, compressing input by factor of 8 with 32 channels. We demonstrate that\n53\treconstruction (PSNR, LPIPS, PESQ, etc.) and generation results on objective\n54\t(Frechet Distance, CLIP score, CLAP score, etc.) and subjective (side-by-side\n55\tevaluation) metrics matches or surpasses frontier opensource tokenizers, such as\n56\tVAEs from Wan-2.2, HunyuanVideo-1.5, FLUX.2, MovieGen, StableAudio and\n57\tMMAudio. Considering difficulty of development, we share with community\n58\ttraining details, model selection method and ablation on design choices. The\n59\tcode is publicly available at https://github.com/kandinskylab/kvae and\n60\thttps://github.com/kandinskylab/kvae-audio.\n61\t\n62\t1\n63\t\n64\tIntroduction\n65\t\n66\tIn recent years development of generative models reached an unprecedented scale with evergrowing\n67\tnumber of theoretical works and applications. Their fields of usage range from synthesizing molecules\n68\t[56] and CT-scans [120] to Earth-weather prediction [9, 70] and autonomous driving [64, 85].\n69\tPinnacle of gradual shift toward adoption of this solutions lies in media content creation, meaning:\n70\taudio [87, 36], images [86, 1] and video [98, 30].\n71\t1\n72\t\n73\tworked on video tokenization\n74\tworked on image tokenization\n75\t3\n76\tworked on audio tokenization\n77\t4\n78\tworked on diffusbility estimation\n79\t2\n80\t\n81\t.\n82\t\n83\t\fFrom technical standpoint, most of this works based on either autoregression or diffusion, including\n84\tones that combine both such as [88]. Consider text-conditioned diffusion-based video generation. This\n85\tscenario differs from popular among researchers class-conditional or unconditional video generation\n86\t(on UCF-101 [84] or SkyTimelapse [109]) in scale of compute, data and overall training difficulty,\n87\tarising due to presence of additional modules, such as text encoder [73, 18, 111]. High dimensinality\n88\tof signal forces corresponding solutions (opensource as well as commercial) to generate videos in\n89\tlatent space following [76, 6]. This space is formed via visual encoder and generated latents are\n90\ttransformed to plain videos through visual decoder, hence these pair encoder-decoder, named visual\n91\ttokenizer, directly influence quality of generations, training dynamics and properties of generative\n92\tmodel. While operating in entirely different space, audio tokenizer plays equally important role and\n93\tshare key characteristics with its visual counterpart.\n94\tAbility of tokenizer to form proper for diffusion latent space got recognized in [82] and was called\n95\tdiffusability. Another important characteristic of tokenizer is compression ratio, since it defines\n96\tnumber of tokens generative model will operate upon. Due to quadratic complexity of attention,\n97\tdevelopment of tokenizers with high compression ratio may be seen as form of optimization [14]\n98\tapart from, for example, quantization [118] or distillation [62]. So by targeting diffusability and high\n99\tcompression ratio simultaneously, one can speed up inference and training, which in turn reduces\n100\tcarbon emissions [17, 44]. Here we present series of novel models for multiple modalities under one\n101\tcommon name KVAE, which matches or surpasses opensource alternatives on both generation and\n102\treconstruction, and also discuss problems, which arise in design process of efficient tokenizers for\n103\tdiffusion.\n104\tFirst sections of report (Sec. 2 - Sec. 5) will be solely devoted to visual domain, which will lay\n105\tdown foundation for audio tokenizer in Sec. 6. It starts with discussion of prior art (Sec. 2):\n106\tevolution of video generation methods; overview of approaches for visual tokenization in image and\n107\tvideo domains; estimates of tokenizer’s diffusability. Then, we provide (Sec. 3) details regarding\n108\tproposed video models: choice of architecture, training strategy and optimization objectives. Content\n109\tof next section focused on comparison of KVAE-3D models with opponnents on reconstruction\n110\t(Sec. 4.1) and generation tasks (Sec. 4.2 - Sec. 4.3), including info on test datasets and overall\n111\tprocedure. Results section ends (Sec. 4.4) with introduction and evaluation of KVAE-2D, an image\n112\ttokenizer. Report continues with remarks on evaluation of diffusability in visual domain (Sec. 5.1)\n113\tand ablatation in design choices (Sec. 5.2), which offer some explanation for final configuration of\n114\tKVAE video tokenizers. Content of last section (Sec. 6) reveals crucial details about novel KVAE\n115\taudio tokenizer. Finally, after report’s conclusion there is supplementary with visual examples and\n116\tadditional comments.\n117\t\n118\t2\n119\t\n120\tRelated Works\n121\t\n122\t2.1\n123\t\n124\tGenerative video models\n125\t\n126\tGenerative adversarial networks. Foundation for modern methods was laid in image domain and\n127\tthis principle holds for [97], where GAN [28] was extended to unconditional video generation\n128\tusing spatio-temporal convolutions and background-foreground decomposition. Later works, such as\n129\tSync-DRAW [61], TGANs-C [66], IRC-GAN [21] set more ambitious goal of generating with text\n130\tcondition, where the latter was encoded by recurrent networks. And results of DVD-GAN [19] only\n131\tconfirmed that significant amount of training data is needed for advesarial setting in order to generate\n132\tconsistent videos. Computational burden of video modelling motivated MoCoGAN-HD [91] to seek\n133\tmotion in latent space of frozen image generator, which speed up training and enable native inference\n134\tfor higher resolutions frames (up to 1024x1024 in the paper). Both StyleGAN-V [83] and [11] not\n135\tonly improved fidelity of generations, but also targeted longer videos, yet they’ve retreated to simpler\n136\ttasks of unconditional generation, which raises scalability questions.\n137\tAutoregression. First attempts, such as VPN [38] adapt PixelCNN [94] and model each R/G/B-color\n138\tpixel by pixel. In order to make this viable, VideoGPT [110] proposed modelling with VQ-VAE\n139\t[95], utilizing its rich codebook of visual representations, and substituted recurrent neural networks\n140\twith Transformer [96] as main computational block, enhancing effective context size. By scaling\n141\tdata and model size CogVideo [35] was able to generate short 480x480 videos from text description.\n142\tWhile this works targeted only video domain, VideoPoet [45] and Emu3 [102] pursued the goal\n143\tof modalities unification under LLM-based pipeline. Generation quality of next-token prediction\n144\t\n145\t2\n146\t\n147\t\fmodels continue to grow and spark interest from large-scale projects, such as GrokImagine [108]\n148\twhere it successfully applied.\n149\tDiffusion. VDM [34] was among first to view text-to-video from conditional diffusion perspective.\n150\tIt was followed by Make-a-video [80], where videos were treated in unsupervised manner, leveraging\n151\texisting text-visual alignment of T2I models. Utilizing cascaded diffusion, Imagen Video [33] set new\n152\tgeneration standard by synthesizing 5 seconds of 24FPS video in HD resolution. While delivering\n153\tstate-of-the-art results, it was still pixel-based, but LDM [77] was eventually extended to video in\n154\tVideo LDM [8] and StableVideo [7]. This latter was able to train text-to-video LDM from scratch\n155\tby dataset scaling, thorough filtering and tree-stage curriculum. Shortly after, unleashing diffusion\n156\tpotential, Sora [12] video generation model was anounced. Since then multitude solutions have\n157\temerged, proprietary (Kling [43], Runway [78], Veo [29]) as well as opensource (Kandinsky [4],\n158\tHunyuanVideo [48], Wan [98], LTX [30]). While high-quality video generation is still challenging\n159\t(e.g. duration), some of this products incorporate synced audio generation [30] or enable precise\n160\tediting [69], pushing boundaries of generative media.\n161\t2.2\n162\t\n163\tVisual tokenizers for LDM\n164\t\n165\tModeling in latent space of VAE [42] was proposed in original LDM work [77] for text-to-image\n166\ttask. This two stage decomposition creates dependency on properties and quality of tokenizer, which\n167\tin turn results in research directions seeking useful modifications, such as: compression ratio [13, 15],\n168\tincorporation of visual foundation model [122, 25], diffusion-based decoders [79, 93]. Meanwhile,\n169\tvideo models were challenged with temporal compression: from Stable Video [6] completely\n170\tdiscarding it to experimental CV-VAE [121], inheriting latent space from pretrained image model,\n171\tand Reducio [90], performing compression with condition on keyframes akin to traditional video\n172\tcodecs. Yet mainstream works, among which are CogVideoX [112], Cosmos [65] and WF-VAE\n173\t[55], are causal tokenizers [115] trained simultaneously on images and videos, capable to compress\n174\tnumber of frames in 4 times at least. Progress in the field highlighted by the fact that every new\n175\tgeneration of popular opensource solutions (Wan [99], Hunyuan [104]) includes updated tokenizer\n176\twith improved metrics, featuring changes in compression ratio and architecture.\n177\t2.3\n178\t\n179\tGenerative performance prediction\n180\t\n181\tA practical bottleneck in LDM development is selecting a visual tokenizer before incurring the cost\n182\tof training a full downstream diffusion model. Reconstruction metrics are available at this stage, but\n183\tthey do not reliably predict downstream generation quality. Yao et al. [113] describe this conflict\n184\tas the reconstruction–generation dilemma: under fixed downstream training compute, increasing\n185\tthe per-token feature dimension can improve reconstruction FID while degrading generative FID.\n186\tTheir VA-VAE aligns latents with vision foundation model features and expands the observed\n187\treconstruction–generation Pareto frontier. These findings motivate the study of latent-space properties\n188\tbeyond reconstruction fidelity.\n189\tDiffusability characterizes how amenable a latent representation is to diffusion modeling, rather than\n190\thow accurately its decoder reconstructs the input [82]. Diffusability should not be conflated with\n191\tgenerative performance itself: it describes a structural property of the latent space, while frequency and\n192\tcorrelation structure are candidate diagnostics for this property rather than its definition. Skorokhodov\n193\tet al. report unusually strong high-frequency components in modern autoencoder latents, especially\n194\tin high-dimensional bottlenecks, and hypothesize that these components interfere with the coarse-tofine bias of diffusion synthesis. Their scale-equivariance regularizer aligns latent- and RGB-space\n195\tfrequency behavior and improves image and video generation after short fine-tuning. Reconstruction\n196\tfidelity, diffusability, and generative performance should therefore be treated as three distinct axes,\n197\tand how well the first two predict the third remains an open question.\n198\tSubsequent work develops more specific spectral hypotheses along two separate axes. For video VAEs,\n199\tSSVAE identifies a spatio-temporal spectrum biased toward low frequencies and, separately, a channel\n200\teigenspectrum concentrated in a small number of modes [58]. These are distinct properties—one\n201\tconcerns spatio-temporal frequency content, the other the distribution of variance across channels—\n202\tand SSVAE’s Local Correlation Regularization and Latent Masked Reconstruction objectives target\n203\tboth. The authors report a 3× acceleration of text-to-video convergence and a 10% improvement in\n204\tvideo reward over the evaluated open-source baselines. Spectrum Matching [63] instead characterizes\n205\t\n206\t3\n207\t\n208\t\fthe target spectrum as flattened but still power-law decaying, rather than simply concentrated at\n209\tlow frequencies, and adds a capacity constraint: this shape must be balanced against information\n210\tpreservation under a fixed bottleneck, since excessive smoothing removes detail whereas excessive\n211\twhitening can impede denoising. Encoding Spectrum Matching constrains the latent PSD toward a\n212\tflattened power-law shape, whereas Decoding Spectrum Matching preserves frequency-to-frequency\n213\tsemantic correspondence through the decoder.\n214\tREPA-E provides complementary end-to-end evidence using two contrasting VAEs [53]. REPA-E\n215\tend-to-end tuning makes the noisy SD-VAE latents smoother and the over-smoothed IN-VAE latents\n216\tmore detailed. By contrast, directly backpropagating the diffusion loss into the VAE encourages\n217\tsimpler latent structures that are easier to denoise but degrade generation quality. Together, these\n218\tstudies suggest a working hypothesis: a generative latent may need enough spectral bias to support\n219\tdiffusion modeling while retaining enough information capacity for faithful reconstruction and\n220\tsynthesis. Diffusability and reconstruction fidelity should therefore be treated as jointly constrained\n221\trequirements rather than a simple trade-off, although the precise mechanism linking spectral bias and\n222\tinformation capacity to generation quality is not yet established.\n223\tThese findings motivate testing inexpensive frozen-tokenizer diagnostics that could plausibly reflect\n224\tthis spectral-bias–information-capacity balance. In Sec. 5.1, we evaluate one such candidate, a spatial\n225\tcorrelation-decay statistic (CDS), and examine its association with downstream generation quality as\n226\tan initial candidate-screening criterion.\n227\t\n228\t3\n229\t\n230\tMethod\n231\t\n232\t3.1\n233\t\n234\tDefinitions\n235\t\n236\tDefine input RGB video as x ∈ R3×T ×H×W , then visual tokenizer is a pair of encoder E compressing\n237\toriginal signal and decoder D reconstructing it. Specifically:\n238\t \\mathcal {E}: R^{3\\times T\\times H\\times W}\\rightarrow R^{c\\times t\\times h\\times w}\\\\ \\\\ \\mathcal {D}: R^{c\\times t\\times h\\times w} \\rightarrow R^{3\\times T\\times H\\times W}\\\\ \\\\ t = \\frac {T-1}{f_t} +1 ;\\quad h = \\frac {H}{f_s} ;\\quad w = \\frac {W}{f_s}\n239\t\n240\tFactors ft , fs and number of hidden channels c are fixed by layers configuration and may serve\n241\tas charachteristic of tokenizer. Here and later, when referencing some tokenizer its compression\n242\tparameters will be put together in short string of form ft × fs × fs (c channels). Namely, this work\n243\tpresents two video models: KVAE–4 × 8 × 8 (16 channels) and KVAE–4 × 16 × 16 (64 channels).\n244\t3.2\n245\t\n246\tArchitecture\n247\t\n248\tWhile video tokenizer’s architecture often appear similar to the image ones, there is also a significant\n249\tdifferences because of the additional temporal axis. First, while input and output convolutions may\n250\tremain 2D [106], deeper layers are either plain Conv3D [112] or decomposed on temporal and\n251\tspatial components [65]. Second, downsample and upsample are also operate on this new axis,\n252\traising issues of compression order and block’s design. Third, targeting video generation progressive\n253\ttraining schedule and final use-cases, such as Image-to-Video, tokenizer should process images\n254\tand videos, which results in causal setup, usually implemented with assymetric temporal padding.\n255\tFourth, attention choice affects training and inference, with options such as full attention [48],\n256\tspatio-temporal [65] and spatial-only [99]. Finally, for long sequences and large resolutions tiling\n257\t(spatial or temporal) almost certainly will be used.\n258\tInsipired by CogVideoX [112], proposed KVAE models are trained to compress long sequences of\n259\tframes. For that reason, their design is attention-free with Conv3D as main computational block. This\n260\tchoice leads to efficient training and ability to perceive arbitrarily long sequences on inference via\n261\tcaching mechanism. As for normalization, both models built upon spatial RMSNorm, which allows\n262\tsegment size adjustment on inference. Motivated by [13, 65] downsample and upsample blocks\n263\t4\n264\t\n265\t\fperform temporal and spatial operations sequentially. Important to note that while Wan-2.2 [99]\n266\tapplies patching as form of compression on earlier layers, KVAE-4x16x16 leaves it for downsample\n267\tblocks.\n268\tAmong visual tokenizers, assymetry in number of parameters between encoder and decoder is\n269\tcommon. For KVAE-4x8x8 it takes moderate form – with more layers decoder is approximately\n270\t1.3× bigger than encoder. But KVAE-4x16x16 takes step further by making decoder layers wider\n271\tand reducing channels in encoder layers. It results in parameters ratio of 5.3×, which is explained by\n272\tnecessity of higher generation capabilities of decoder.\n273\t3.3\n274\t\n275\tTraining methodology\n276\t\n277\tSince production-grade solutions target generation of 10 seconds videos with 24FPS in FullHD\n278\tresolution, tokenizer should perceive such inputs reliably. For that reason on training we adapt\n279\tsequence length scaling approach from CogVideoX [112]. But instead of starting with 17 frames, it\n280\tbegins with 65 frames, which throughout our experiments led to faster convergence. Moreover, we\n281\tfound that limiting number of frames during training by 129 is enough and models generalize well to\n282\tlonger sequences (up to 400 on test), allowing larger batch size than with original limit of 161 frames.\n283\tWhile causal architecture already allows to perceive single frame, during training video batches were\n284\tinterleaved with images with batch sampling probability of 0.3.\n285\tTraining for both models is divided into 4 stages defined by their objectives. On first stage sum of 3\n286\tcomponents is considered:\n287\t  L^I = \\mathcal {L}_1(x, \\mathcal {D}(\\mathcal {E}(x)_s)) + w_{\\text {perc}}\\cdot \\text {LPIPS}(x, \\mathcal {D}(\\mathcal {E}(x)_s)) + w_{\\text {KL}}\\cdot D_{KL}(\\mathcal {E}(x), \\mathcal {N}(0, I)) \n288\twhere L1 is a Mean Average Error, LPIPS [119] is well-known perceptual loss, DKL is KullbackLeibler divergence and E(x)s denotes sample from normal distribution with parameters E(x). Second\n289\tstage introduces frame-based GAN [22] with few thousand warm-up steps for stability:\n290\t  L^{II} = L^I + w_{adv}\\cdot \\text {GAN}(\\mathcal {D}(\\mathcal {E}(x)_s), x) \n291\tThird stage adapt EQ-loss [51] on top of LII with latent downsampling, rotation and last stage is\n292\tdecoder finetuning. Objectives are optimized by Adam [41] with learning rates being fixed for every\n293\tstage.\n294\tTraining dataset for tokenizer comprises of 10 million images and 2 million videos, which all were\n295\tchosen from pretrain dataset of generative model. Accounting for only a few percentages of total data,\n296\tits content already filtered by resolution, motion and content. Preserving characteristics of original\n297\tsignal, we limit preprocessing transformations to flip, rotation, resize and crop with resolution varying\n298\tacross the stages from 256 to 512.\n299\t\n300\t4\n301\t\n302\tResults\n303\t\n304\t4.1\n305\t\n306\tReconstruction\n307\t\n308\tMain purpose of tokenizers is to provide latent space for generative models. And, as was shown in\n309\tseries of works regarding image domain [114, 122], excellent reconstructions does not necessarily\n310\talign with high generation results. Yet, since proposed models were trained under reconstruction\n311\tobjectives, corresponding metrics would indicate their ability to build visual representations and\n312\tabsence of artifacts after decoding.\n313\tTable 1: Reconstruction results for tokenizers on MCL-JCV 720p, grouped by compression factor\n314\tmodel\n315\tfactor\n316\tchannels PSNR SSIM LPIPS\n317\tHunyuanVideo-1.0\n318\tWan-2.1\n319\tKVAE-2.0\n320\t\n321\t4x8x8\n322\t\n323\t16\n324\t\n325\t34.3\n326\t34.3\n327\t36.0\n328\t\n329\t0.90\n330\t0.89\n331\t0.92\n332\t\n333\t0.047\n334\t0.044\n335\t0.047\n336\t\n337\tHunyuanVideo-1.5\n338\tWan-2.2\n339\tKVAE-2.0\n340\t\n341\t4×16×16\n342\t\n343\t32\n344\t48\n345\t64\n346\t\n347\t34.4\n348\t34.2\n349\t35.2\n350\t\n351\t0.89\n352\t0.89\n353\t0.91\n354\t\n355\t0.073\n356\t0.037\n357\t0.058\n358\t\n359\t5\n360\t\n361\t\fWe compare against leading opensource methods, such as HunyuanVideo and Wan, on MCLJCV[100], chosen as primary source of test video sequences, because of resolution and length in\n362\tframes. Sequences were trimmed until total amount of frames can be expressed in form 8 × k + 1,\n363\twhere k ∈ N. Since HunyaunVideo tokenizers using full attention, on long sequences tiling was\n364\tapplied to their inputs and latents. As for Wan and KVAE – no modifications to the original forward\n365\tprocedure were made. And as can be seen in Table 1, proposed models gain additional PSNR (on\n366\taverage higher than 1 dB) with negligible LPIPS loss.\n367\tDespite the proposals for tokenizer’s dataset, such as TokenBench in [65], there is still no common\n368\ttest procedure. So by composing few open datasets, such as AOM-CTC [3], BVI-DVC [59] and\n369\tothers, we harvested a few hunderds videos varying by their content, resolution and motion (see Fig.\n370\t1). Being divided by categories, this dataset allowed extensive evaluation during development of the\n371\tmodels.\n372\t\n373\tFigure 1: Frames from internal video dataset used for intermediate evaluations\n374\t\n375\t4.2\n376\t\n377\tImage generation\n378\t\n379\tFollowing procedure described in Kandinsky-5 [4] training of video generation model starts from\n380\ttext-to-image stage on low resolution. This model is built upon CrossDiT architecure, utilizing\n381\tQwen-2.5VL [5] for text embeddings and minimizes flow matching loss. All image and video\n382\texperiments were conducted on 2B generation model. Because visual encoders considered here have\n383\tdifferent compression factors, it is possible to alter patch size, balancing amount of input visual\n384\ttokens. Specifically, for KVAE-4x8x8 patch size of 2x2 is set, while to all 4x16x16 models patch 1x1\n385\tis applied. With same dataset (of few hundred millions images), captions and hyperparameters, the\n386\tfinal results are expected to depend only on visual tokenizers.\n387\tGenerations are evaluated by CLIP [73] and FID [32] every 10K steps of optimizer, see Fig. 2\n388\tfor learning dynamic. From it a conclusion can be made that tokenizers with higher compression\n389\tfactors bring faster convergence. Increasing number of channels in KVAE-4x16x16 up to 64 provided\n390\tbenefits, such as surpassing FID results of HunyuanVideo-1.5 with 32 channels, which raises an open\n391\tquestion on relation between compression ratio and generative performance.\n392\tIn addition to CLIP and FID, for final checkpoints side-by-side evaluation is performed on rich set of\n393\tprompts, ranging across styles (photorealism, futuristic, cartoon, etc.), scenery (interior, outdoors and\n394\t6\n395\t\n396\t\fFigure 2: Learning curves of text-to-image generation models on 384x256 resolution\n397\tmore), subjects (their number, appearance). For each pair, generated from same prompt, annotator\n398\tmark preferable image independently for three categories: prompt following, visual quality and\n399\tsemantic quality. If it is impossible to decide, then there is draw options (both good or both bad) and\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "7a35efc4-b1fe-475d-bfd8-9a4d6bf7f723", "uuid": "57e23367-2fff-4175-8931-9a0d4707ef74", "timestamp": "2026-08-07T05:34:38.285Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/kVAE_paper.txt", "content": "KVAE: Family of Tokenizers for Multimodal\nGenerative Models\n\narXiv:2608.05798v1 [cs.CV] 6 Aug 2026\n\nAndrey Shutkin1\nKandinsky Lab\n\nDenis Parkhomenko1, 2, 3, 4\nKandinsky Lab\n\nKirill Malakhov2, 4\nKandinsky Lab\n\nIlia Vasiliev2, 4\nKandinsky Lab\n\nDavid Chikovani2, 4\nKandinsky Lab\n\nAlexander Ivanov3\nKandinsky Lab\n\nIvan Kirillov3\nKandinsky Lab\nIlia Trushkin2\nKandinsky Lab\n\nIvan Mikheev1\nKandinsky Lab\n\nAzat Saginbaev3\nKandinsky Lab\n\nKirill Chernyshev1\nKandinsky Lab\nValeriya Kobenko2\nKandinsky Lab\nEgor Silvestrov3\nKandinsky Lab\n\nKonstantin Zakharov2\nKandinsky Lab\n\nAbstract\nLatent diffusion modeling (LDM), a prominent paradigm, utilizes tokenizers to\nmap input signal to compressed representation. This dependency positions tokenizer as an integral part of generation process itself, since it affects learning\nspeed, quality of synthesized samples and lay foundation for later applications.\nThis report presents series of KVAE tokenizers for audio, image and video, all\ndesigned for subsequent text-conditioned generation: KVAE-Audio, a continuous\nfull-band 48 kHz tokenizer with a 50 Hz latent of 64 channels; KVAE-3D – two\ncausal video tokenizers for 4x16x16 and 4x8x8 compression; KVAE-2D, an image\nmodel, compressing input by factor of 8 with 32 channels. We demonstrate that\nreconstruction (PSNR, LPIPS, PESQ, etc.) and generation results on objective\n(Frechet Distance, CLIP score, CLAP score, etc.) and subjective (side-by-side\nevaluation) metrics matches or surpasses frontier opensource tokenizers, such as\nVAEs from Wan-2.2, HunyuanVideo-1.5, FLUX.2, MovieGen, StableAudio and\nMMAudio. Considering difficulty of development, we share with community\ntraining details, model selection method and ablation on design choices. The\ncode is publicly available at https://github.com/kandinskylab/kvae and\nhttps://github.com/kandinskylab/kvae-audio.\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nIn recent years development of generative models reached an unprecedented scale with evergrowing\nnumber of theoretical works and applications. Their fields of usage range from synthesizing molecules\n[56] and CT-scans [120] to Earth-weather prediction [9, 70] and autonomous driving [64, 85].\nPinnacle of gradual shift toward adoption of this solutions lies in media content creation, meaning:\naudio [87, 36], images [86, 1] and video [98, 30].\n1\n\nworked on video tokenization\nworked on image tokenization\n3\nworked on audio tokenization\n4\nworked on diffusbility estimation\n2\n\n.\n\n\fFrom technical standpoint, most of this works based on either autoregression or diffusion, including\nones that combine both such as [88]. Consider text-conditioned diffusion-based video generation. This\nscenario differs from popular among researchers class-conditional or unconditional video generation\n(on UCF-101 [84] or SkyTimelapse [109]) in scale of compute, data and overall training difficulty,\narising due to presence of additional modules, such as text encoder [73, 18, 111]. High dimensinality\nof signal forces corresponding solutions (opensource as well as commercial) to generate videos in\nlatent space following [76, 6]. This space is formed via visual encoder and generated latents are\ntransformed to plain videos through visual decoder, hence these pair encoder-decoder, named visual\ntokenizer, directly influence quality of generations, training dynamics and properties of generative\nmodel. While operating in entirely different space, audio tokenizer plays equally important role and\nshare key characteristics with its visual counterpart.\nAbility of tokenizer to form proper for diffusion latent space got recognized in [82] and was called\ndiffusability. Another important characteristic of tokenizer is compression ratio, since it defines\nnumber of tokens generative model will operate upon. Due to quadratic complexity of attention,\ndevelopment of tokenizers with high compression ratio may be seen as form of optimization [14]\napart from, for example, quantization [118] or distillation [62]. So by targeting diffusability and high\ncompression ratio simultaneously, one can speed up inference and training, which in turn reduces\ncarbon emissions [17, 44]. Here we present series of novel models for multiple modalities under one\ncommon name KVAE, which matches or surpasses opensource alternatives on both generation and\nreconstruction, and also discuss problems, which arise in design process of efficient tokenizers for\ndiffusion.\nFirst sections of report (Sec. 2 - Sec. 5) will be solely devoted to visual domain, which will lay\ndown foundation for audio tokenizer in Sec. 6. It starts with discussion of prior art (Sec. 2):\nevolution of video generation methods; overview of approaches for visual tokenization in image and\nvideo domains; estimates of tokenizer’s diffusability. Then, we provide (Sec. 3) details regarding\nproposed video models: choice of architecture, training strategy and optimization objectives. Content\nof next section focused on comparison of KVAE-3D models with opponnents on reconstruction\n(Sec. 4.1) and generation tasks (Sec. 4.2 - Sec. 4.3), including info on test datasets and overall\nprocedure. Results section ends (Sec. 4.4) with introduction and evaluation of KVAE-2D, an image\ntokenizer. Report continues with remarks on evaluation of diffusability in visual domain (Sec. 5.1)\nand ablatation in design choices (Sec. 5.2), which offer some explanation for final configuration of\nKVAE video tokenizers. Content of last section (Sec. 6) reveals crucial details about novel KVAE\naudio tokenizer. Finally, after report’s conclusion there is supplementary with visual examples and\nadditional comments.\n\n2\n\nRelated Works\n\n2.1\n\nGenerative video models\n\nGenerative adversarial networks. Foundation for modern methods was laid in image domain and\nthis principle holds for [97], where GAN [28] was extended to unconditional video generation\nusing spatio-temporal convolutions and background-foreground decomposition. Later works, such as\nSync-DRAW [61], TGANs-C [66], IRC-GAN [21] set more ambitious goal of generating with text\ncondition, where the latter was encoded by recurrent networks. And results of DVD-GAN [19] only\nconfirmed that significant amount of training data is needed for advesarial setting in order to generate\nconsistent videos. Computational burden of video modelling motivated MoCoGAN-HD [91] to seek\nmotion in latent space of frozen image generator, which speed up training and enable native inference\nfor higher resolutions frames (up to 1024x1024 in the paper). Both StyleGAN-V [83] and [11] not\nonly improved fidelity of generations, but also targeted longer videos, yet they’ve retreated to simpler\ntasks of unconditional generation, which raises scalability questions.\nAutoregression. First attempts, such as VPN [38] adapt PixelCNN [94] and model each R/G/B-color\npixel by pixel. In order to make this viable, VideoGPT [110] proposed modelling with VQ-VAE\n[95], utilizing its rich codebook of visual representations, and substituted recurrent neural networks\nwith Transformer [96] as main computational block, enhancing effective context size. By scaling\ndata and model size CogVideo [35] was able to generate short 480x480 videos from text description.\nWhile this works targeted only video domain, VideoPoet [45] and Emu3 [102] pursued the goal\nof modalities unification under LLM-based pipeline. Generation quality of next-token prediction\n\n2\n\n\fmodels continue to grow and spark interest from large-scale projects, such as GrokImagine [108]\nwhere it successfully applied.\nDiffusion. VDM [34] was among first to view text-to-video from conditional diffusion perspective.\nIt was followed by Make-a-video [80], where videos were treated in unsupervised manner, leveraging\nexisting text-visual alignment of T2I models. Utilizing cascaded diffusion, Imagen Video [33] set new\ngeneration standard by synthesizing 5 seconds of 24FPS video in HD resolution. While delivering\nstate-of-the-art results, it was still pixel-based, but LDM [77] was eventually extended to video in\nVideo LDM [8] and StableVideo [7]. This latter was able to train text-to-video LDM from scratch\nby dataset scaling, thorough filtering and tree-stage curriculum. Shortly after, unleashing diffusion\npotential, Sora [12] video generation model was anounced. Since then multitude solutions have\nemerged, proprietary (Kling [43], Runway [78], Veo [29]) as well as opensource (Kandinsky [4],\nHunyuanVideo [48], Wan [98], LTX [30]). While high-quality video generation is still challenging\n(e.g. duration), some of this products incorporate synced audio generation [30] or enable precise\nediting [69], pushing boundaries of generative media.\n2.2\n\nVisual tokenizers for LDM\n\nModeling in latent space of VAE [42] was proposed in original LDM work [77] for text-to-image\ntask. This two stage decomposition creates dependency on properties and quality of tokenizer, which\nin turn results in research directions seeking useful modifications, such as: compression ratio [13, 15],\nincorporation of visual foundation model [122, 25], diffusion-based decoders [79, 93]. Meanwhile,\nvideo models were challenged with temporal compression: from Stable Video [6] completely\ndiscarding it to experimental CV-VAE [121], inheriting latent space from pretrained image model,\nand Reducio [90], performing compression with condition on keyframes akin to traditional video\ncodecs. Yet mainstream works, among which are CogVideoX [112], Cosmos [65] and WF-VAE\n[55], are causal tokenizers [115] trained simultaneously on images and videos, capable to compress\nnumber of frames in 4 times at least. Progress in the field highlighted by the fact that every new\ngeneration of popular opensource solutions (Wan [99], Hunyuan [104]) includes updated tokenizer\nwith improved metrics, featuring changes in compression ratio and architecture.\n2.3\n\nGenerative performance prediction\n\nA practical bottleneck in LDM development is selecting a visual tokenizer before incurring the cost\nof training a full downstream diffusion model. Reconstruction metrics are available at this stage, but\nthey do not reliably predict downstream generation quality. Yao et al. [113] describe this conflict\nas the reconstruction–generation dilemma: under fixed downstream training compute, increasing\nthe per-token feature dimension can improve reconstruction FID while degrading generative FID.\nTheir VA-VAE aligns latents with vision foundation model features and expands the observed\nreconstruction–generation Pareto frontier. These findings motivate the study of latent-space properties\nbeyond reconstruction fidelity.\nDiffusability characterizes how amenable a latent representation is to diffusion modeling, rather than\nhow accurately its decoder reconstructs the input [82]. Diffusability should not be conflated with\ngenerative performance itself: it describes a structural property of the latent space, while frequency and\ncorrelation structure are candidate diagnostics for this property rather than its definition. Skorokhodov\net al. report unusually strong high-frequency components in modern autoencoder latents, especially\nin high-dimensional bottlenecks, and hypothesize that these components interfere with the coarse-tofine bias of diffusion synthesis. Their scale-equivariance regularizer aligns latent- and RGB-space\nfrequency behavior and improves image and video generation after short fine-tuning. Reconstruction\nfidelity, diffusability, and generative performance should therefore be treated as three distinct axes,\nand how well the first two predict the third remains an open question.\nSubsequent work develops more specific spectral hypotheses along two separate axes. For video VAEs,\nSSVAE identifies a spatio-temporal spectrum biased toward low frequencies and, separately, a channel\neigenspectrum concentrated in a small number of modes [58]. These are distinct properties—one\nconcerns spatio-temporal frequency content, the other the distribution of variance across channels—\nand SSVAE’s Local Correlation Regularization and Latent Masked Reconstruction objectives target\nboth. The authors report a 3× acceleration of text-to-video convergence and a 10% improvement in\nvideo reward over the evaluated open-source baselines. Spectrum Matching [63] instead characterizes\n\n3\n\n\fthe target spectrum as flattened but still power-law decaying, rather than simply concentrated at\nlow frequencies, and adds a capacity constraint: this shape must be balanced against information\npreservation under a fixed bottleneck, since excessive smoothing removes detail whereas excessive\nwhitening can impede denoising. Encoding Spectrum Matching constrains the latent PSD toward a\nflattened power-law shape, whereas Decoding Spectrum Matching preserves frequency-to-frequency\nsemantic correspondence through the decoder.\nREPA-E provides complementary end-to-end evidence using two contrasting VAEs [53]. REPA-E\nend-to-end tuning makes the noisy SD-VAE latents smoother and the over-smoothed IN-VAE latents\nmore detailed. By contrast, directly backpropagating the diffusion loss into the VAE encourages\nsimpler latent structures that are easier to denoise but degrade generation quality. Together, these\nstudies suggest a working hypothesis: a generative latent may need enough spectral bias to support\ndiffusion modeling while retaining enough information capacity for faithful reconstruction and\nsynthesis. Diffusability and reconstruction fidelity should therefore be treated as jointly constrained\nrequirements rather than a simple trade-off, although the precise mechanism linking spectral bias and\ninformation capacity to generation quality is not yet established.\nThese findings motivate testing inexpensive frozen-tokenizer diagnostics that could plausibly reflect\nthis spectral-bias–information-capacity balance. In Sec. 5.1, we evaluate one such candidate, a spatial\ncorrelation-decay statistic (CDS), and examine its association with downstream generation quality as\nan initial candidate-screening criterion.\n\n3\n\nMethod\n\n3.1\n\nDefinitions\n\nDefine input RGB video as x ∈ R3×T ×H×W , then visual tokenizer is a pair of encoder E compressing\noriginal signal and decoder D reconstructing it. Specifically:\n \\mathcal {E}: R^{3\\times T\\times H\\times W}\\rightarrow R^{c\\times t\\times h\\times w}\\\\ \\\\ \\mathcal {D}: R^{c\\times t\\times h\\times w} \\rightarrow R^{3\\times T\\times H\\times W}\\\\ \\\\ t = \\frac {T-1}{f_t} +1 ;\\quad h = \\frac {H}{f_s} ;\\quad w = \\frac {W}{f_s}\n\nFactors ft , fs and number of hidden channels c are fixed by layers configuration and may serve\nas charachteristic of tokenizer. Here and later, when referencing some tokenizer its compression\nparameters will be put together in short string of form ft × fs × fs (c channels). Namely, this work\npresents two video models: KVAE–4 × 8 × 8 (16 channels) and KVAE–4 × 16 × 16 (64 channels).\n3.2\n\nArchitecture\n\nWhile video tokenizer’s architecture often appear similar to the image ones, there is also a significant\ndifferences because of the additional temporal axis. First, while input and output convolutions may\nremain 2D [106], deeper layers are either plain Conv3D [112] or decomposed on temporal and\nspatial components [65]. Second, downsample and upsample are also operate on this new axis,\nraising issues of compression order and block’s design. Third, targeting video generation progressive\ntraining schedule and final use-cases, such as Image-to-Video, tokenizer should process images\nand videos, which results in causal setup, usually implemented with assymetric temporal padding.\nFourth, attention choice affects training and inference, with options such as full attention [48],\nspatio-temporal [65] and spatial-only [99]. Finally, for long sequences and large resolutions tiling\n(spatial or temporal) almost certainly will be used.\nInsipired by CogVideoX [112], proposed KVAE models are trained to compress long sequences of\nframes. For that reason, their design is attention-free with Conv3D as main computational block. This\nchoice leads to efficient training and ability to perceive arbitrarily long sequences on inference via\ncaching mechanism. As for normalization, both models built upon spatial RMSNorm, which allows\nsegment size adjustment on inference. Motivated by [13, 65] downsample and upsample blocks\n4\n\n\fperform temporal and spatial operations sequentially. Important to note that while Wan-2.2 [99]\napplies patching as form of compression on earlier layers, KVAE-4x16x16 leaves it for downsample\nblocks.\nAmong visual tokenizers, assymetry in number of parameters between encoder and decoder is\ncommon. For KVAE-4x8x8 it takes moderate form – with more layers decoder is approximately\n1.3× bigger than encoder. But KVAE-4x16x16 takes step further by making decoder layers wider\nand reducing channels in encoder layers. It results in parameters ratio of 5.3×, which is explained by\nnecessity of higher generation capabilities of decoder.\n3.3\n\nTraining methodology\n\nSince production-grade solutions target generation of 10 seconds videos with 24FPS in FullHD\nresolution, tokenizer should perceive such inputs reliably. For that reason on training we adapt\nsequence length scaling approach from CogVideoX [112]. But instead of starting with 17 frames, it\nbegins with 65 frames, which throughout our experiments led to faster convergence. Moreover, we\nfound that limiting number of frames during training by 129 is enough and models generalize well to\nlonger sequences (up to 400 on test), allowing larger batch size than with original limit of 161 frames.\nWhile causal architecture already allows to perceive single frame, during training video batches were\ninterleaved with images with batch sampling probability of 0.3.\nTraining for both models is divided into 4 stages defined by their objectives. On first stage sum of 3\ncomponents is considered:\n  L^I = \\mathcal {L}_1(x, \\mathcal {D}(\\mathcal {E}(x)_s)) + w_{\\text {perc}}\\cdot \\text {LPIPS}(x, \\mathcal {D}(\\mathcal {E}(x)_s)) + w_{\\text {KL}}\\cdot D_{KL}(\\mathcal {E}(x), \\mathcal {N}(0, I)) \nwhere L1 is a Mean Average Error, LPIPS [119] is well-known perceptual loss, DKL is KullbackLeibler divergence and E(x)s denotes sample from normal distribution with parameters E(x). Second\nstage introduces frame-based GAN [22] with few thousand warm-up steps for stability:\n  L^{II} = L^I + w_{adv}\\cdot \\text {GAN}(\\mathcal {D}(\\mathcal {E}(x)_s), x) \nThird stage adapt EQ-loss [51] on top of LII with latent downsampling, rotation and last stage is\ndecoder finetuning. Objectives are optimized by Adam [41] with learning rates being fixed for every\nstage.\nTraining dataset for tokenizer comprises of 10 million images and 2 million videos, which all were\nchosen from pretrain dataset of generative model. Accounting for only a few percentages of total data,\nits content already filtered by resolution, motion and content. Preserving characteristics of original\nsignal, we limit preprocessing transformations to flip, rotation, resize and crop with resolution varying\nacross the stages from 256 to 512.\n\n4\n\nResults\n\n4.1\n\nReconstruction\n\nMain purpose of tokenizers is to provide latent space for generative models. And, as was shown in\nseries of works regarding image domain [114, 122], excellent reconstructions does not necessarily\nalign with high generation results. Yet, since proposed models were trained under reconstruction\nobjectives, corresponding metrics would indicate their ability to build visual representations and\nabsence of artifacts after decoding.\nTable 1: Reconstruction results for tokenizers on MCL-JCV 720p, grouped by compression factor\nmodel\nfactor\nchannels PSNR SSIM LPIPS\nHunyuanVideo-1.0\nWan-2.1\nKVAE-2.0\n\n4x8x8\n\n16\n\n34.3\n34.3\n36.0\n\n0.90\n0.89\n0.92\n\n0.047\n0.044\n0.047\n\nHunyuanVideo-1.5\nWan-2.2\nKVAE-2.0\n\n4×16×16\n\n32\n48\n64\n\n34.4\n34.2\n35.2\n\n0.89\n0.89\n0.91\n\n0.073\n0.037\n0.058\n\n5\n\n\fWe compare against leading opensource methods, such as HunyuanVideo and Wan, on MCLJCV[100], chosen as primary source of test video sequences, because of resolution and length in\nframes. Sequences were trimmed until total amount of frames can be expressed in form 8 × k + 1,\nwhere k ∈ N. Since HunyaunVideo tokenizers using full attention, on long sequences tiling was\napplied to their inputs and latents. As for Wan and KVAE – no modifications to the original forward\nprocedure were made. And as can be seen in Table 1, proposed models gain additional PSNR (on\naverage higher than 1 dB) with negligible LPIPS loss.\nDespite the proposals for tokenizer’s dataset, such as TokenBench in [65], there is still no common\ntest procedure. So by composing few open datasets, such as AOM-CTC [3], BVI-DVC [59] and\nothers, we harvested a few hunderds videos varying by their content, resolution and motion (see Fig.\n1). Being divided by categories, this dataset allowed extensive evaluation during development of the\nmodels.\n\nFigure 1: Frames from internal video dataset used for intermediate evaluations\n\n4.2\n\nImage generation\n\nFollowing procedure described in Kandinsky-5 [4] training of video generation model starts from\ntext-to-image stage on low resolution. This model is built upon CrossDiT architecure, utilizing\nQwen-2.5VL [5] for text embeddings and minimizes flow matching loss. All image and video\nexperiments were conducted on 2B generation model. Because visual encoders considered here have\ndifferent compression factors, it is possible to alter patch size, balancing amount of input visual\ntokens. Specifically, for KVAE-4x8x8 patch size of 2x2 is set, while to all 4x16x16 models patch 1x1\nis applied. With same dataset (of few hundred millions images), captions and hyperparameters, the\nfinal results are expected to depend only on visual tokenizers.\nGenerations are evaluated by CLIP [73] and FID [32] every 10K steps of optimizer, see Fig. 2\nfor learning dynamic. From it a conclusion can be made that tokenizers with higher compression\nfactors bring faster convergence. Increasing number of channels in KVAE-4x16x16 up to 64 provided\nbenefits, such as surpassing FID results of HunyuanVideo-1.5 with 32 channels, which raises an open\nquestion on relation between compression ratio and generative performance.\nIn addition to CLIP and FID, for final checkpoints side-by-side evaluation is performed on rich set of\nprompts, ranging across styles (photorealism, futuristic, cartoon, etc.), scenery (interior, outdoors and\n6\n\n\fFigure 2: Learning curves of text-to-image generation models on 384x256 resolution\nmore), subjects (their number, appearance). For each pair, generated from same prompt, annotator\nmark preferable image independently for three categories: prompt following, visual quality and\nsemantic quality. If it is impossible to decide, then there is draw options (both good or both bad) and", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 0, "totalLines": 1811}}}
