{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b653919dea06840d", "type": "tool_result", "content": "400\tsemantic quality. If it is impossible to decide, then there is draw options (both good or both bad) and\n401\texact formula for win rate is:\n402\t  \\text {WinRate}(A) = \\frac {\\text {win}_A + 0.5 \\cdot \\text {both good}}{\\text {win}_A + \\text {win}_B + \\text {both good}} \n403\tSurprisingly, for KVAE-4x8x8 (see Fig. 4) objective metrics contradict with side-by-side evaluation,\n404\twhere it holds leading positions. At the same time, KVAE-4x16x16 chosen in total accordance with\n405\tits metrics (see Fig. 3): gives up visual appearance over prompt following in comparison to Wan-2.2\n406\tand consistenly preferred over HunyuanVideo-1.5 on all categories.\n407\t\n408\t(a) Comparison with HunyuanVideo-1.5\n409\t\n410\t(b) Comparison with Wan-2.2\n411\t\n412\tFigure 3: Results of side-by-side evaluation of image generations for KVAE-4x16x16\n413\t\n414\t(a) Comparison with HunyuanVideo-1.5\n415\t\n416\t(b) Comparison with Wan-2.2\n417\t\n418\tFigure 4: Results of side-by-side evaluation of image generations for KVAE-4x8x8\n419\t4.3\n420\t\n421\tVideo generation\n422\t\n423\tImage checkpoint serves as starting point for video generation training, which progressively increase\n424\tresolution across stages. To monitor intermediate quality of generated videos we evaluate such\n425\tmetrics as text-to-video correspondence through InternVideo2 [103], overall visual appearance with\n426\tQAlign [105] and dynamics estimation based on VideoMAE2 features [101, 49]. Comparison of\n427\t\n428\t7\n429\t\n430\t\fHunyaunVideo-1.5 VAE and KVAE-4x16x16 during validation on resolution 384x256 are provided\n431\ton Fig. 6 and results of side-by-side evaluation on resolution 768x512 are present in Fig. 5.\n432\t\n433\tFigure 5: Side-by-side evaluation of 121-frame 768x512 video generations for KVAE-4x16x16 and\n434\tHunyuanVideo-1.5\n435\tFor text prompts we utilize MovieGen Benchmark [69], where each query is enriched with object\n436\tdetails and motion description, allowing to gather a set of diverse videos from almost thousand\n437\tprompts. Results of this experiments allow to conclude that under Kandinsky-5 training pipeline,\n438\tfeaturing large amount of filtered data, several stages and valid multimodal architecture, KVAE4x16x16 form better latent space than HunyuanVideo-1.5 VAE, which eventually led to faster\n439\tconvergence and better generations.\n440\t\n441\tFigure 6: Learning curves of text-to-video generation models on 121-frame 384x256 resolution. With\n442\thigher values of QAlign and InterVideo2 scores, training with KVAE-4x16x16 is also characterized\n443\tby less variance across this three metrics.\n444\t4.4\n445\t\n446\tKVAE-2D-2.0 results\n447\t\n448\tTo complement the video-tokenizer results, we evaluate KVAE-2D-2.0, the final image tokenizer used\n449\tin the image-domain diffusability analysis of Sec. 5.1. Architecturally, it is a four-level convolutional\n450\tresidual autoencoder with base width 128 and channel multipliers (1, 2, 4, 8). It maps images to a\n451\t32-channel Gaussian latent with 8x8 spatial compression.\n452\tWe first evaluate tokenizer reconstruction on OmniDoc-TokenBench, a 3042-image benchmark of\n453\ttext-rich documents [72, 2]. Table 2 reports KVAE-2D-2.0 surpassing published FLUX.1-dev and\n454\tFLUX.2-dev baselines on PSNR, SSIM and NED.\n455\tTable 2: OmniDoc-TokenBench reconstruction results. FLUX.1-dev and FLUX.2-dev are published\n456\tthird-party baselines.\n457\tMetric\n458\t\n459\tKVAE-2D-2.0 (ours, 8x8, 32 channels)\n460\t\n461\tFLUX.1-dev (published, 8x8, 16 channels)\n462\t\n463\tFLUX.2-dev (published, 8x8, 32 channels)\n464\t\n465\tPSNR ↑\n466\tSSIM ↑\n467\tLPIPS ↓\n468\tFID ↓\n469\tNED ↑\n470\t\n471\t28.05\n472\t0.957\n473\t0.028\n474\t1.74\n475\t0.976\n476\t\n477\t26.24\n478\t0.9364\n479\t0.0247\n480\t0.5543\n481\t0.9546\n482\t\n483\t27.72\n484\t0.9544\n485\t0.0216\n486\t0.73\n487\t0.9535\n488\t\n489\t8\n490\t\n491\t\fMotivated by the strong public T2I performance of FLUX.2 reported in DiffusionBench [54], we use\n492\tits native VAE as the reference tokenizer. We compare generations from two downstream 2B T2I DiT\n493\tcheckpoints after 200K optimization steps: one stack uses the FLUX.2 VAE, and the other uses the\n494\t400K-step KVAE-2D-2.0 checkpoint. The point estimates favor the KVAE-2D-2.0 stack in all three\n495\tcriteria, with the largest difference in semantic quality (5.3 percentage points).\n496\t\n497\tFigure 7: Side-by-side soft win rates for the two downstream 2B T2I DiT stacks at 200K optimization\n498\tsteps.\n499\t\n500\t5\n501\t\n502\tAnalysis\n503\t\n504\tDuring development phase a number of design choices were filtered out, mainly based on quality of\n505\tgeneration models trained on top of corresponding tokenizers. This process eventually led to final\n506\tweights of KVAE-2D-2.0, KVAE-4x16x16 and KVAE-4x8x8. Below we provide details regarding\n507\tdiffusability estimation, as a way to select best tokenizers prior to generation model training, and\n508\tcomparison with alternative versions of KVAE, confirming superiority of proposed models.\n509\t5.1\n510\t\n511\tDiffusability\n512\t\n513\tScreening setup. Building on Sec. 2.3, we examine associations between inexpensive measurements\n514\tof a frozen tokenizer and the visual quality of generations produced by a downstream DiT. We\n515\tevaluated spatial self-similarity statistics, semantic probes, spectral and PCA-based diagnostics,\n516\tdrawing on iREPA [81], SSVAE [58], and Spectrum Matching [63]. This section focuses on\n517\tCorrelation Decay Slope (CDS), the primary spatial statistic retained for model selection, and reports\n518\tits behavior and supporting evidence.\n519\tSubjective target. The target is the visual-quality criterion from human side-by-side evaluations. We\n520\taggregate pairwise outcomes with a Bradley–Terry model [10], treating both good and both bad as\n521\tone half-win for each system. This differs from the descriptive win rate in Sec. 4.2- 4.4, which assigns\n522\tone half-win to both good but excludes both bad. The analysis spans 14 tokenizer configurations; the\n523\tBradley–Terry refit incorporates the KVAE-2D-2.0 versus FLUX.2 comparison reported in Sec. 4.4.\n524\tThe resulting target captures subjective downstream visual quality rather than reconstruction fidelity.\n525\tCorrelation Decay Slope. Let zx,p ∈ RC denote the latent feature vector for image x at spatial\n526\tposition p. Using cosine similarity, we define the spatial correlogram and CDS as:\n527\t  g(\\delta )=\\mathbb {E}_{x,p,q}\\Big [\\tfrac {\\langle z_{x,p},z_{x,q}\\rangle }{\\Vert z_{x,p}\\Vert _2\\Vert z_{x,q}\\Vert _2}\\,\\Big |\\,\\Vert p-q\\Vert _1=\\delta \\Big ], \\qquad \\mathrm {CDS}=-\\beta . \n528\twhere the expectation averages over images and valid spatial pairs, δ is Manhattan distance on the\n529\tlatent grid, and β comes from the fit g(δ) ≃ α + βδ over δ = 1, . . . , 8 (default iREPA protocol). The\n530\tcross-sectional panel uses ImageNet-val full50k at 256 px. Larger CDS therefore indicates faster\n531\tspatial decorrelation. The figures show correlograms through δ = 16, whereas all reported CDS\n532\tvalues use the δ = 1, . . . , 8 fit.\n533\tCDS was introduced by iREPA for patch-token representations [81] and later applied to VAE latents\n534\tin Diffusing in the Right Space [123]. Across the 14 image-tokenizer configurations, CDS has\n535\tPearson correlation r = 0.906 with the Bradley–Terry visual-quality score (Fig. 8a). This in9\n536\t\n537\t\fsample association motivates its use for candidate screening, but it does not establish out-of-sample\n538\tprediction.\n539\tCDS during joint VAE–DiT training. We next examine whether CDS evolves in the direction suggested by the cross-sectional association. A single trajectory optimized with a REPA-E representationalignment objective [53] is evaluated on ImageNet-val 10k using the same CDS definition. During\n540\tthis run, the correlogram becomes steeper (Figs. 8b and 8c), and CDS increases from 0.0429 at\n541\tinitialization to 0.0518 at 400k steps. This one trajectory does not provide checkpoint-level validation\n542\tagainst subjective quality and should not be interpreted as a general training law.\n543\tImage-tokenizer comparison. Fig. 8d compares three 32-channel 8x8 image tokenizers: VF, a\n544\tvision-foundation-aligned model motivated by VA-VAE [113]; NoReg, trained with reconstruction\n545\tand KL losses; and the final KVAE-2D-2.0 tokenizer. The curves differ in both their absolute cosinesimilarity levels and their rates of decay with distance. Among these models, NoReg has the highest\n546\tPSNR and the lowest Bradley–Terry visual-quality score, illustrating why reconstruction alone is\n547\tinsufficient for tokenizer selection. The decay rate provides information not captured by a single\n548\tabsolute similarity value; CDS summarizes this early-range decay over δ = 1, . . . , 8.\n549\t\n550\t(a) BT quality vs CDS.\n551\t\n552\t(b) Joint-train correlograms.\n553\t\n554\t(c) CDS vs joint step.\n555\t\n556\t(d) Tokenizer correlograms.\n557\t\n558\tFigure 8: CDS analysis for image tokenizers: (a) Bradley–Terry visual quality vs CDS (n=14;\n559\tKVAE-2D-2.0 highlighted); (b)–(c) correlogram and CDS along one joint VAE–DiT trajectory; (d)\n560\tspatial correlograms for VF, NoReg (reconstruction+KL), and KVAE-2D-2.0.\n561\tDiffusability and CDS, used as a proxy for generation performance, were crucial for training and\n562\tselecting the best image VAEs. The same approach was adopted for audio and video tokenizers. It\n563\thelped identify effective designs while reducing computational cost.\n564\t5.2\n565\t\n566\tDesign choices\n567\t\n568\tOriginal CogVideoX VAE [112] utilized GroupNorm as normalization layer across all architecture.\n569\tSince statistics were computed across all axis, including time, normalization violated causality.\n570\tMoreover, during context parallel training complete computation of statistics required all-to-all\n571\tsynchronization. Replacing it with RMSNorm, which operate only spatially, eliminates this problems\n572\t\n573\t10\n574\t\n575\t\fand does not increase complexity. It can be seen (Fig. 9) that both models obey same learning\n576\tdynamic, which allows us to switch to more practical RMSNorm without quality loss.\n577\t\n578\tFigure 9: Normalization layer comparison on KVAE-4x8x8.\n579\tWidth of decoder layers in KVAE-4x16x16 was increased, which affects decoding complexity and,\n580\ttherefore, requires justification. For that purpose version with same width in encoder and decoder\n581\twas trained (see blue curve on Fig. 10) and tested in image generation setting (exactly as in Sec. 4.2).\n582\tFollowing training strategy of KVAE-4x16x16, it reaches higher reconstruction metrics, but lose pace\n583\tunder generation scenario, which is evident from gFID and CLIP-score plots.\n584\tWhile KVAE-4x16x16 is a 64 channel model, there is an argument against such large value. Main\n585\tconcern is amount of steps for generation model’s loss to converge, and should be noted that other\n586\topen models [99, 104] have smaller channel size. To ablate this decision we train KVAE-4x16x16\n587\twith channels reduced down to 32 (see green curve on Fig. 10). As expected, higher compression\n588\tratio leads to worse reconstruction with difference on validation up to 1dB of PSNR and 0.01 of\n589\tLPIPS. But, what’s more important, generation model trained with it fails to catch up with 64 channel\n590\tbaseline. Visual quality is consistently lags behind and CLIP-scores are close for second half of\n591\ttraining, even reaching same values for both models on final steps.\n592\t\n593\tFigure 10: Ablation study for the 4x16x16 design. Left/Middle images: reconstruction metrics\n594\tRight image: learning curves of text-to-video generation models\n595\t\n596\t6\n597\t\n598\tAudio tokenization\n599\t\n600\tKey principles of visual tokenization outlined in previous section holds for audio as well: latent diffusion is the dominant recipe for text-to-audio and joint audio-video generation, the generative model\n601\tnever observes a waveform, and the properties of the latent bound both what it can express and how\n602\tfast it converges. What changes are the constraints. This section presents KVAE-Audio, a continuous\n603\tfull-band audio tokenizer, together with the design decisions and evaluation protocol behind it. Inference code is publicly available under the MIT license at https://github.com/kandinskylab/\n604\tkvae-audio, with weights released at https://huggingface.co/kandinskylab/KVAE-Audio.\n605\t\n606\t11\n607\t\n608\t\fTwo requirements shaped the design. The first is full-band operation. A large share of open audio\n609\ttokenizers either operates at 16–24 kHz, or compresses a mel-spectrogram and delegates waveform\n610\tsynthesis to a separate vocoder [57, 16]. Both routes cap the usable bandwidth well below the audible\n611\trange, and the latter additionally discards phase at the input and spends compute on an extra model\n612\tat decoding time. For media production, where generated audio accompanies video and is mixed\n613\twith speech, music and sound effects in a single track, this is unacceptable, so we model waveforms\n614\tend-to-end at 48 kHz. This matters beyond audio alone: joint text-to-video-and-audio generation\n615\tcurrently has no open solution competitive with closed ones, and a full-band tokenizer is a prerequisite\n616\trather than a refinement.\n617\tThe second requirement is the diffusability of the latent in the sense of Sec. 2.3: as in the visual\n618\tdomain, reconstruction quality is a poor predictor of downstream generation quality, and the two\n619\tcan be traded against each other. We adapted the correlation decay slope of Sec. 5.1 to the onedimensional audio latent and use it as a cheap screening signal when selecting candidates, but the final\n620\tchoice among them is always made after training a generative model on top of the frozen tokenizer\n621\t(Sec. 6.6). The generative model is therefore part of the evaluation protocol rather than a downstream\n622\tconsumer of it.\n623\tThe section is structured as follows. Relevant work on audio tokenization is reviewed first (Sec. 6.1),\n624\tfollowed by definitions, architecture and training methodology of the proposed model (Sec. 6.2 –\n625\tSec. 6.4). Comparison with alternative solutions on reconstruction (Sec. 6.5) and generation (Sec. 6.6)\n626\tfollows, including test data and evaluation procedure, and the section closes with ablations on the\n627\tdesign choices behind the final configuration (Sec. 6.7).\n628\t6.1\n629\t\n630\tAudio tokenizers for latent diffusion\n631\t\n632\tNeural codecs. The convolutional encoder-decoder with residual vector quantization, introduced\n633\tby SoundStream [117] and refined by EnCodec [20] and DAC [52], is the architectural ancestor\n634\tof most modern audio tokenizers. These models are optimized for transmission: the discrete code\n635\tstructure and low bitrate that make a codec good make it a poor latent space for diffusion, which is\n636\twhy generative pipelines built on them replace quantization with a continuous bottleneck.\n637\tMel-spectrogram tokenizers. An alternative line compresses mel-spectrograms rather than waveforms and restores the signal with a separate vocoder, following AudioLDM [57]. The autoencoder\n638\tof MMAudio [16], widely used in open-source text-to-audio-video pipelines, belongs to this family:\n639\ta 1D convolutional VAE over mel-spectrograms producing a 40-channel latent at 43.07 Hz for\n640\t44.1 kHz input, decoded back to spectrograms and vocoded by BigVGAN-v2 [27]. Same route\n641\tis taken by LTX-2 [30] for the audio branch of a joint audio-visual model, encoding two-channel\n642\tmel-spectrograms at 16 kHz into 128-dimensional tokens at 25 Hz. Phase is discarded at the input\n643\tand the achievable bandwidth is bounded by the vocoder, which places these models in a different\n644\tfidelity regime from end-to-end waveform tokenizers.\n645\tContinuous waveform tokenizers. Closest to our setting are continuous VAEs operating directly on\n646\twaveforms and serving as the latent space of text-to-audio and video-to-audio models. Several of\n647\tthem share our starting point: MovieGen [69], HunyuanVideo-Foley [89] and Qwen-Audio-VAE [71]\n648\tall replace the residual quantizer of DAC with a Gaussian bottleneck, arriving at 128-dimensional\n649\tlatents at 25 Hz (48 kHz input), 50 Hz (48 kHz) and 12.5 Hz (24 kHz) respectively, the last with\n650\ta causal stack and windowed Transformers at the bottleneck for streaming use. The Stable Audio\n651\tline takes a different architectural route: the Stable Audio Open VAE [23] is convolutional with\n652\ta 64-dimensional latent at 21.5 Hz, while SAME [68], the autoencoder of Stable Audio 3 [24], is\n653\tTransformer-based and reaches 4096× compression — roughly 10.8 Hz — with a 256-dimensional\n654\tlatent over stereo 44.1 kHz input. Reported configurations therefore span 24–48 kHz in sample rate,\n655\tan order of magnitude in latent frame rate, and 64–256 channels, at broadly comparable reconstruction\n656\tquality; the choice of operating point is a design question rather than a matter of scale.\n657\tRepresentation alignment. Aligning latents or intermediate features to frozen foundation models\n658\thas become a standard tool for improving diffusion training in the image domain [116, 113, 122].\n659\tAudio has seen analogous attempts, most of them aligning the diffusion transformer rather than the\n660\ttokenizer, and most of them targeting self-supervised speech representations [89]. The closest work\n661\tto ours is SAME [68], which regularizes the autoencoder itself: semantic regression onto frozen\n662\t\n663\t12\n664\t\n665\t\frepresentations, combined with a small diffusion transformer trained jointly on the latent so that its\n666\tgradients shape the encoder. We explore alignment on the tokenizer side as well; see Sec. 6.3.\n667\t6.2\n668\t\n669\tDefinitions and architecture\n670\t\n671\tDefine the input waveform as x ∈ RC×L , with C channels of L samples at a sample rate sr. The\n672\taudio tokenizer is a pair of encoder E and decoder D:\n673\t \\mathcal {E}: R^{C \\times L} \\rightarrow R^{d \\times l} \\\\ \\\\ \\mathcal {D}: R^{d \\times l} \\rightarrow R^{C \\times L} \\\\ \\\\ l = \\frac {L}{f_t}; \\quad f_t = \\prod _{i} s_i\n674\t\n675\twhere ft is the temporal compression factor, obtained as the product of layer strides si , and d is\n676\tthe number of latent channels. Unlike the visual case, no spatial axis is present, so a tokenizer is\n677\tcharacterized by the triple (sample rate, latent frame rate sr/ft , channels d). KVAE-Audio operates\n678\tat 48 kHz with a 50 Hz latent of 64 channels, i.e. ft = 960 and one latent frame per 20 ms of audio.\n679\tKVAE-Audio inherits its convolutional backbone from DAC [52]: residual units with dilated convolutions followed by strided convolutions that compress the signal in time and widen it in channels, with\n680\ta mirrored decoder built on transposed convolutions. We keep the Snake activation x + sin2 (αx)/α,\n681\twhose periodic nature is the right inductive bias for oscillatory signals and suppresses tonal artifacts.\n682\tRelative to the original codec we introduce the following changes.\n683\tContinuous bottleneck. The residual vector quantizer and its codebooks are replaced by a reparameterization module predicting the parameters of a Gaussian posterior and sampling a continuous vector\n684\tfrom it. This is the change that converts the codec into a VAE, and it is what makes the model usable\n685\tas a tokenizer for latent diffusion at all: diffusion in a continuous space does not benefit from the\n686\tdiscrete code structure a transmission codec is optimized for.\n687\tStride ladder and depth. One block is added to both encoder and decoder, and the stride schedule\n688\tis re-factorized to [2, 3, 4, 5, 8] instead of the original [2, 4, 8, 8], with additional projections at the\n689\tencoder output and the decoder input. The product 960 places the latent at exactly 50 Hz for a 48\n690\tkHz input, against the roughly 86 Hz of the original codec. Compared to a shorter ladder with larger\n691\tindividual strides, the additional stage buys compression without forcing single blocks to cover a\n692\treceptive field their residual units cannot support.\n693\tLatent channels. The latent carries 64 channels, against 1024 in the codec it derives from and\n694\t40–256 in the continuous tokenizers we compare against. The channel count is the sharpest trade-off\n695\tin the whole design and is ablated in Sec. 6.7.\n696\tAttention in the bottleneck. A single self-attention block operates on the 50 Hz sequence before the\n697\tlatent projection. At this frame rate attention is inexpensive — one second of audio is 50 tokens —\n698\tand it lets the latent rely on context well beyond the local convolutional window.\n699\t6.3\n700\t\n701\tLatent representation alignment\n702\t\n703\tA recurring theme across all modalities in this report is that a tokenizer trained purely for reconstruction does not necessarily produce a latent space that is easy for a generative model to learn, and\n704\tthat aligning representations to a frozen foundation model is one way to improve it (Sec. 5.1). For\n705\tKVAE-Audio we regularize the encoder output towards the representation of a frozen audio foundation model, having searched over the choice of that model, the layer the target is taken from, and\n706\tthe mechanism bringing the two representations into correspondence. The configuration we settled\n707\ton improves diffusability, and with it prompt following, without a noticeable cost in reconstruction\n708\tquality.\n709\t\n710\t13\n711\t\n712\t\f6.4\n713\t\n714\tTraining methodology\n715\t\n716\tObjectives. Training combines a waveform-domain reconstruction term, a multi-scale spectral term,\n717\tKL regularization of the posterior, an adversarial term, an auxiliary perceptual term computed on\n718\tfrozen audio features, and the alignment regularizer of Sec. 6.3:\n719\t  \\begin {split} L = \\; & \\mathcal {L}_1(x, \\hat {x}) + w_{\\text {spec}} \\cdot \\mathcal {L}_{\\text {spec}}(x, \\hat {x}) + w_{\\text {KL}} \\cdot D_{KL}(\\mathcal {E}(x), \\mathcal {N}(0, I)) \\\\ & + w_{\\text {adv}} \\cdot \\text {GAN}(\\hat {x}, x) + w_{\\text {perc}} \\cdot \\mathcal {L}_{\\text {perc}}(x, \\hat {x}) + w_{\\text {align}} \\cdot \\mathcal {L}_{\\text {align}}(\\mathcal {E}(x), \\mathcal {F}(x)) \\end {split} \n720\twhere x̂ = D(E(x)s ) and Lspec is a sum of L1 distances between log-magnitude mel-spectrograms\n721\tcomputed at several window sizes [52]. In the last term F denotes a frozen audio foundation model\n722\tproviding the target representation and Lalign the correspondence between it and the latent; the identity\n723\tof F and the form of Lalign are deferred to the dedicated publication, as stated in Sec. 6.3. Both wperc\n724\tand walign are set so that neither dominates the spectral term, which we found to be the fastest way to\n725\tlose high-frequency detail: the two act on the same signal from different directions, and the useful\n726\trange for their sum is narrower than for either alone. All objectives are optimized by Adam [41] with\n727\tlearning rates fixed within each stage.\n728\tDiscriminators. Adversarial supervision is provided by an ensemble of discriminators of the kind\n729\testablished in the neural codec and vocoder literature — period-based discriminators operating on\n730\treshaped waveform segments [46] together with spectral discriminators at several STFT resolutions [52]. We compared several such configurations, varying both the composition of the ensemble\n731\tand the resolutions it covers. The comparison confirmed the general finding of that literature: no\n732\tsingle discriminator family is sufficient on its own, since each is sensitive to a different class of\n733\tartifact, and combinations covering both the temporal and the spectral view behave more stably in the\n734\tlate stages of training, when the remaining errors are concentrated in the upper band.\n735\tPerceptual objectives. For Lperc we compared several perceptual losses computed on frozen audio\n736\trepresentations, differing in the backbone providing the features, the depth at which they are taken,\n737\tand how the distance between them is formed. Perceptual terms of this kind interact strongly with the\n738\trest of the objective: they are effective at removing the artifacts that spectral distances tolerate, but a\n739\tweight high enough to change the character of the reconstruction also pulls the model away from\n740\tfaithful high-frequency detail, so the useful range is narrow and has to be found empirically for a\n741\tgiven backbone — and, as noted above, jointly with the alignment weight rather than in isolation.\n742\tThe configuration used for the released checkpoints follows from this comparison together with the\n743\talignment study of Sec. 6.3, and is left for the dedicated publication.\n744\tCrop length schedule. Following the sequence-length scaling used for the video tokenizers (Sec. 3),\n745\tearly stages train on 0.38-second crops and later stages increase the crop length to 5 seconds. Short\n746\tcrops maximize the number of independent examples per batch and carry the model cheaply through\n747\tthe bulk of convergence; long crops matter for two reasons specific to audio. They expose the model\n748\tto slow envelopes — reverberation tails, sustained tones, musical phrase structure — that do not fit\n749\tinto a short window at all, and they bring the training distribution closer to the segment lengths the\n750\ttokenizer will see when serving a generative model.\n751\tDecoder fine-tuning. The final stage freezes the encoder and fine-tunes the decoder alone. This is\n752\tclose to a free lunch: the latent is fixed at that point, so reconstruction improves without invalidating\n753\ta generative model already trained on that latent space, and the tokenizer can keep improving after\n754\tthe generative training has started.\n755\tData. Training data combines open and proprietary sources spanning speech, music and general\n756\tsound events. One filter is worth stating explicitly: a substantial fraction of nominally 48 kHz audio\n757\tin the wild is upsampled from lower rates and carries no energy in the upper band. Training a\n758\tfull-band tokenizer on such material teaches it to reproduce an empty spectrum, so we estimate the\n759\ttrue bandwidth of every file and filter accordingly. After filtering, the training set amounts to roughly\n760\t10 thousand hours.\n761\t6.5\n762\t\n763\tReconstruction\n764\t\n765\tThere is no commonly accepted benchmark for audio tokenizers, and, unlike in the visual domain,\n766\tthe relevant content spans domains with incompatible characteristics — a model that reconstructs\n767\tspeech well may fail on dense polyphonic music or on broadband transients. We therefore evaluate on\n768\tthree public test sets, one per domain: AudioSet eval [26] for general sound, MUSDB18-HQ [74] for\n769\t14\n770\t\n771\t\fmusic, and EARS [75] for speech, the last being natively 48 kHz with many speakers and expressive\n772\tstyles.\n773\tReported metrics cover three aspects. Spectral distances — STFT and mel, each averaged over\n774\ttwo window configurations and computed on linear and logarithmic magnitudes — measure the\n775\tperceptually relevant error. A sample-wise waveform distance is reported alongside them because it is\n776\tthe only one of the three that is sensitive to phase, which the spectral distances ignore by construction\n777\tand which a waveform tokenizer is meant to preserve. Signal-level ratios (SI-SDR, SDR, SNR)\n778\tquantify distortion energy, with SI-SDR invariant to overall level. For speech we additionally report\n779\tPESQ-WB [37].\n780\tIn all reconstruction experiments the encoder output is taken as the posterior mean rather than a sample\n781\tfrom it, for our model and for every baseline alike. Sampling would add a stochastic component that\n782\treflects the strength of the KL regularization more than the quality of the representation, and it is the\n783\tmean that a generative model is trained to predict.\n784\tBaselines are open tokenizers with released weights, evaluated by us under an identical protocol:\n785\tthe 44.1 kHz MMAudio VAE [16], chosen as the primary baseline because it is the autoencoder\n786\tmost often found under open text-to-audio-video systems; the DAC-VAE of MovieGen Audio [69],\n787\tchosen because, like ours, it derives from DAC; and SAME-L [68], the autoencoder of Stable Audio\n788\t3 [24]. Since the MMAudio VAE and SAME-L operate at 44.1 kHz, all reconstructions — ours and\n789\tMovieGen’s included — are resampled to 44.1 kHz before the metrics are computed to make the\n790\tcomparison fair.\n791\tTab. 3 reports the outcome. KVAE-Audio leads on spectral distances on general audio and music and\n792\tis within noise of the best model on speech, while using 64 latent channels against 128 and 256 for the\n793\ttwo waveform baselines, and 166.9M parameters against 852.1M for SAME-L. The MMAudio VAE,\n794\tbeing mel-based, shows the expected signature of a model that does not preserve phase: competitive\n795\tspectral distances alongside negative SI-SDR.\n796\tTable 3: Reconstruction results by domain, all metrics computed at 44.1 kHz. Best in bold, second\n797\tunderlined.\n798\t(a) AudioSet eval\n799\tModel\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "7a35efc4-b1fe-475d-bfd8-9a4d6bf7f723", "uuid": "a4389d7a-fc12-40ce-abfd-df5014aba1c9", "timestamp": "2026-08-07T05:36:17.080Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/kVAE_paper.txt", "content": "semantic quality. If it is impossible to decide, then there is draw options (both good or both bad) and\nexact formula for win rate is:\n  \\text {WinRate}(A) = \\frac {\\text {win}_A + 0.5 \\cdot \\text {both good}}{\\text {win}_A + \\text {win}_B + \\text {both good}} \nSurprisingly, for KVAE-4x8x8 (see Fig. 4) objective metrics contradict with side-by-side evaluation,\nwhere it holds leading positions. At the same time, KVAE-4x16x16 chosen in total accordance with\nits metrics (see Fig. 3): gives up visual appearance over prompt following in comparison to Wan-2.2\nand consistenly preferred over HunyuanVideo-1.5 on all categories.\n\n(a) Comparison with HunyuanVideo-1.5\n\n(b) Comparison with Wan-2.2\n\nFigure 3: Results of side-by-side evaluation of image generations for KVAE-4x16x16\n\n(a) Comparison with HunyuanVideo-1.5\n\n(b) Comparison with Wan-2.2\n\nFigure 4: Results of side-by-side evaluation of image generations for KVAE-4x8x8\n4.3\n\nVideo generation\n\nImage checkpoint serves as starting point for video generation training, which progressively increase\nresolution across stages. To monitor intermediate quality of generated videos we evaluate such\nmetrics as text-to-video correspondence through InternVideo2 [103], overall visual appearance with\nQAlign [105] and dynamics estimation based on VideoMAE2 features [101, 49]. Comparison of\n\n7\n\n\fHunyaunVideo-1.5 VAE and KVAE-4x16x16 during validation on resolution 384x256 are provided\non Fig. 6 and results of side-by-side evaluation on resolution 768x512 are present in Fig. 5.\n\nFigure 5: Side-by-side evaluation of 121-frame 768x512 video generations for KVAE-4x16x16 and\nHunyuanVideo-1.5\nFor text prompts we utilize MovieGen Benchmark [69], where each query is enriched with object\ndetails and motion description, allowing to gather a set of diverse videos from almost thousand\nprompts. Results of this experiments allow to conclude that under Kandinsky-5 training pipeline,\nfeaturing large amount of filtered data, several stages and valid multimodal architecture, KVAE4x16x16 form better latent space than HunyuanVideo-1.5 VAE, which eventually led to faster\nconvergence and better generations.\n\nFigure 6: Learning curves of text-to-video generation models on 121-frame 384x256 resolution. With\nhigher values of QAlign and InterVideo2 scores, training with KVAE-4x16x16 is also characterized\nby less variance across this three metrics.\n4.4\n\nKVAE-2D-2.0 results\n\nTo complement the video-tokenizer results, we evaluate KVAE-2D-2.0, the final image tokenizer used\nin the image-domain diffusability analysis of Sec. 5.1. Architecturally, it is a four-level convolutional\nresidual autoencoder with base width 128 and channel multipliers (1, 2, 4, 8). It maps images to a\n32-channel Gaussian latent with 8x8 spatial compression.\nWe first evaluate tokenizer reconstruction on OmniDoc-TokenBench, a 3042-image benchmark of\ntext-rich documents [72, 2]. Table 2 reports KVAE-2D-2.0 surpassing published FLUX.1-dev and\nFLUX.2-dev baselines on PSNR, SSIM and NED.\nTable 2: OmniDoc-TokenBench reconstruction results. FLUX.1-dev and FLUX.2-dev are published\nthird-party baselines.\nMetric\n\nKVAE-2D-2.0 (ours, 8x8, 32 channels)\n\nFLUX.1-dev (published, 8x8, 16 channels)\n\nFLUX.2-dev (published, 8x8, 32 channels)\n\nPSNR ↑\nSSIM ↑\nLPIPS ↓\nFID ↓\nNED ↑\n\n28.05\n0.957\n0.028\n1.74\n0.976\n\n26.24\n0.9364\n0.0247\n0.5543\n0.9546\n\n27.72\n0.9544\n0.0216\n0.73\n0.9535\n\n8\n\n\fMotivated by the strong public T2I performance of FLUX.2 reported in DiffusionBench [54], we use\nits native VAE as the reference tokenizer. We compare generations from two downstream 2B T2I DiT\ncheckpoints after 200K optimization steps: one stack uses the FLUX.2 VAE, and the other uses the\n400K-step KVAE-2D-2.0 checkpoint. The point estimates favor the KVAE-2D-2.0 stack in all three\ncriteria, with the largest difference in semantic quality (5.3 percentage points).\n\nFigure 7: Side-by-side soft win rates for the two downstream 2B T2I DiT stacks at 200K optimization\nsteps.\n\n5\n\nAnalysis\n\nDuring development phase a number of design choices were filtered out, mainly based on quality of\ngeneration models trained on top of corresponding tokenizers. This process eventually led to final\nweights of KVAE-2D-2.0, KVAE-4x16x16 and KVAE-4x8x8. Below we provide details regarding\ndiffusability estimation, as a way to select best tokenizers prior to generation model training, and\ncomparison with alternative versions of KVAE, confirming superiority of proposed models.\n5.1\n\nDiffusability\n\nScreening setup. Building on Sec. 2.3, we examine associations between inexpensive measurements\nof a frozen tokenizer and the visual quality of generations produced by a downstream DiT. We\nevaluated spatial self-similarity statistics, semantic probes, spectral and PCA-based diagnostics,\ndrawing on iREPA [81], SSVAE [58], and Spectrum Matching [63]. This section focuses on\nCorrelation Decay Slope (CDS), the primary spatial statistic retained for model selection, and reports\nits behavior and supporting evidence.\nSubjective target. The target is the visual-quality criterion from human side-by-side evaluations. We\naggregate pairwise outcomes with a Bradley–Terry model [10], treating both good and both bad as\none half-win for each system. This differs from the descriptive win rate in Sec. 4.2- 4.4, which assigns\none half-win to both good but excludes both bad. The analysis spans 14 tokenizer configurations; the\nBradley–Terry refit incorporates the KVAE-2D-2.0 versus FLUX.2 comparison reported in Sec. 4.4.\nThe resulting target captures subjective downstream visual quality rather than reconstruction fidelity.\nCorrelation Decay Slope. Let zx,p ∈ RC denote the latent feature vector for image x at spatial\nposition p. Using cosine similarity, we define the spatial correlogram and CDS as:\n  g(\\delta )=\\mathbb {E}_{x,p,q}\\Big [\\tfrac {\\langle z_{x,p},z_{x,q}\\rangle }{\\Vert z_{x,p}\\Vert _2\\Vert z_{x,q}\\Vert _2}\\,\\Big |\\,\\Vert p-q\\Vert _1=\\delta \\Big ], \\qquad \\mathrm {CDS}=-\\beta . \nwhere the expectation averages over images and valid spatial pairs, δ is Manhattan distance on the\nlatent grid, and β comes from the fit g(δ) ≃ α + βδ over δ = 1, . . . , 8 (default iREPA protocol). The\ncross-sectional panel uses ImageNet-val full50k at 256 px. Larger CDS therefore indicates faster\nspatial decorrelation. The figures show correlograms through δ = 16, whereas all reported CDS\nvalues use the δ = 1, . . . , 8 fit.\nCDS was introduced by iREPA for patch-token representations [81] and later applied to VAE latents\nin Diffusing in the Right Space [123]. Across the 14 image-tokenizer configurations, CDS has\nPearson correlation r = 0.906 with the Bradley–Terry visual-quality score (Fig. 8a). This in9\n\n\fsample association motivates its use for candidate screening, but it does not establish out-of-sample\nprediction.\nCDS during joint VAE–DiT training. We next examine whether CDS evolves in the direction suggested by the cross-sectional association. A single trajectory optimized with a REPA-E representationalignment objective [53] is evaluated on ImageNet-val 10k using the same CDS definition. During\nthis run, the correlogram becomes steeper (Figs. 8b and 8c), and CDS increases from 0.0429 at\ninitialization to 0.0518 at 400k steps. This one trajectory does not provide checkpoint-level validation\nagainst subjective quality and should not be interpreted as a general training law.\nImage-tokenizer comparison. Fig. 8d compares three 32-channel 8x8 image tokenizers: VF, a\nvision-foundation-aligned model motivated by VA-VAE [113]; NoReg, trained with reconstruction\nand KL losses; and the final KVAE-2D-2.0 tokenizer. The curves differ in both their absolute cosinesimilarity levels and their rates of decay with distance. Among these models, NoReg has the highest\nPSNR and the lowest Bradley–Terry visual-quality score, illustrating why reconstruction alone is\ninsufficient for tokenizer selection. The decay rate provides information not captured by a single\nabsolute similarity value; CDS summarizes this early-range decay over δ = 1, . . . , 8.\n\n(a) BT quality vs CDS.\n\n(b) Joint-train correlograms.\n\n(c) CDS vs joint step.\n\n(d) Tokenizer correlograms.\n\nFigure 8: CDS analysis for image tokenizers: (a) Bradley–Terry visual quality vs CDS (n=14;\nKVAE-2D-2.0 highlighted); (b)–(c) correlogram and CDS along one joint VAE–DiT trajectory; (d)\nspatial correlograms for VF, NoReg (reconstruction+KL), and KVAE-2D-2.0.\nDiffusability and CDS, used as a proxy for generation performance, were crucial for training and\nselecting the best image VAEs. The same approach was adopted for audio and video tokenizers. It\nhelped identify effective designs while reducing computational cost.\n5.2\n\nDesign choices\n\nOriginal CogVideoX VAE [112] utilized GroupNorm as normalization layer across all architecture.\nSince statistics were computed across all axis, including time, normalization violated causality.\nMoreover, during context parallel training complete computation of statistics required all-to-all\nsynchronization. Replacing it with RMSNorm, which operate only spatially, eliminates this problems\n\n10\n\n\fand does not increase complexity. It can be seen (Fig. 9) that both models obey same learning\ndynamic, which allows us to switch to more practical RMSNorm without quality loss.\n\nFigure 9: Normalization layer comparison on KVAE-4x8x8.\nWidth of decoder layers in KVAE-4x16x16 was increased, which affects decoding complexity and,\ntherefore, requires justification. For that purpose version with same width in encoder and decoder\nwas trained (see blue curve on Fig. 10) and tested in image generation setting (exactly as in Sec. 4.2).\nFollowing training strategy of KVAE-4x16x16, it reaches higher reconstruction metrics, but lose pace\nunder generation scenario, which is evident from gFID and CLIP-score plots.\nWhile KVAE-4x16x16 is a 64 channel model, there is an argument against such large value. Main\nconcern is amount of steps for generation model’s loss to converge, and should be noted that other\nopen models [99, 104] have smaller channel size. To ablate this decision we train KVAE-4x16x16\nwith channels reduced down to 32 (see green curve on Fig. 10). As expected, higher compression\nratio leads to worse reconstruction with difference on validation up to 1dB of PSNR and 0.01 of\nLPIPS. But, what’s more important, generation model trained with it fails to catch up with 64 channel\nbaseline. Visual quality is consistently lags behind and CLIP-scores are close for second half of\ntraining, even reaching same values for both models on final steps.\n\nFigure 10: Ablation study for the 4x16x16 design. Left/Middle images: reconstruction metrics\nRight image: learning curves of text-to-video generation models\n\n6\n\nAudio tokenization\n\nKey principles of visual tokenization outlined in previous section holds for audio as well: latent diffusion is the dominant recipe for text-to-audio and joint audio-video generation, the generative model\nnever observes a waveform, and the properties of the latent bound both what it can express and how\nfast it converges. What changes are the constraints. This section presents KVAE-Audio, a continuous\nfull-band audio tokenizer, together with the design decisions and evaluation protocol behind it. Inference code is publicly available under the MIT license at https://github.com/kandinskylab/\nkvae-audio, with weights released at https://huggingface.co/kandinskylab/KVAE-Audio.\n\n11\n\n\fTwo requirements shaped the design. The first is full-band operation. A large share of open audio\ntokenizers either operates at 16–24 kHz, or compresses a mel-spectrogram and delegates waveform\nsynthesis to a separate vocoder [57, 16]. Both routes cap the usable bandwidth well below the audible\nrange, and the latter additionally discards phase at the input and spends compute on an extra model\nat decoding time. For media production, where generated audio accompanies video and is mixed\nwith speech, music and sound effects in a single track, this is unacceptable, so we model waveforms\nend-to-end at 48 kHz. This matters beyond audio alone: joint text-to-video-and-audio generation\ncurrently has no open solution competitive with closed ones, and a full-band tokenizer is a prerequisite\nrather than a refinement.\nThe second requirement is the diffusability of the latent in the sense of Sec. 2.3: as in the visual\ndomain, reconstruction quality is a poor predictor of downstream generation quality, and the two\ncan be traded against each other. We adapted the correlation decay slope of Sec. 5.1 to the onedimensional audio latent and use it as a cheap screening signal when selecting candidates, but the final\nchoice among them is always made after training a generative model on top of the frozen tokenizer\n(Sec. 6.6). The generative model is therefore part of the evaluation protocol rather than a downstream\nconsumer of it.\nThe section is structured as follows. Relevant work on audio tokenization is reviewed first (Sec. 6.1),\nfollowed by definitions, architecture and training methodology of the proposed model (Sec. 6.2 –\nSec. 6.4). Comparison with alternative solutions on reconstruction (Sec. 6.5) and generation (Sec. 6.6)\nfollows, including test data and evaluation procedure, and the section closes with ablations on the\ndesign choices behind the final configuration (Sec. 6.7).\n6.1\n\nAudio tokenizers for latent diffusion\n\nNeural codecs. The convolutional encoder-decoder with residual vector quantization, introduced\nby SoundStream [117] and refined by EnCodec [20] and DAC [52], is the architectural ancestor\nof most modern audio tokenizers. These models are optimized for transmission: the discrete code\nstructure and low bitrate that make a codec good make it a poor latent space for diffusion, which is\nwhy generative pipelines built on them replace quantization with a continuous bottleneck.\nMel-spectrogram tokenizers. An alternative line compresses mel-spectrograms rather than waveforms and restores the signal with a separate vocoder, following AudioLDM [57]. The autoencoder\nof MMAudio [16], widely used in open-source text-to-audio-video pipelines, belongs to this family:\na 1D convolutional VAE over mel-spectrograms producing a 40-channel latent at 43.07 Hz for\n44.1 kHz input, decoded back to spectrograms and vocoded by BigVGAN-v2 [27]. Same route\nis taken by LTX-2 [30] for the audio branch of a joint audio-visual model, encoding two-channel\nmel-spectrograms at 16 kHz into 128-dimensional tokens at 25 Hz. Phase is discarded at the input\nand the achievable bandwidth is bounded by the vocoder, which places these models in a different\nfidelity regime from end-to-end waveform tokenizers.\nContinuous waveform tokenizers. Closest to our setting are continuous VAEs operating directly on\nwaveforms and serving as the latent space of text-to-audio and video-to-audio models. Several of\nthem share our starting point: MovieGen [69], HunyuanVideo-Foley [89] and Qwen-Audio-VAE [71]\nall replace the residual quantizer of DAC with a Gaussian bottleneck, arriving at 128-dimensional\nlatents at 25 Hz (48 kHz input), 50 Hz (48 kHz) and 12.5 Hz (24 kHz) respectively, the last with\na causal stack and windowed Transformers at the bottleneck for streaming use. The Stable Audio\nline takes a different architectural route: the Stable Audio Open VAE [23] is convolutional with\na 64-dimensional latent at 21.5 Hz, while SAME [68], the autoencoder of Stable Audio 3 [24], is\nTransformer-based and reaches 4096× compression — roughly 10.8 Hz — with a 256-dimensional\nlatent over stereo 44.1 kHz input. Reported configurations therefore span 24–48 kHz in sample rate,\nan order of magnitude in latent frame rate, and 64–256 channels, at broadly comparable reconstruction\nquality; the choice of operating point is a design question rather than a matter of scale.\nRepresentation alignment. Aligning latents or intermediate features to frozen foundation models\nhas become a standard tool for improving diffusion training in the image domain [116, 113, 122].\nAudio has seen analogous attempts, most of them aligning the diffusion transformer rather than the\ntokenizer, and most of them targeting self-supervised speech representations [89]. The closest work\nto ours is SAME [68], which regularizes the autoencoder itself: semantic regression onto frozen\n\n12\n\n\frepresentations, combined with a small diffusion transformer trained jointly on the latent so that its\ngradients shape the encoder. We explore alignment on the tokenizer side as well; see Sec. 6.3.\n6.2\n\nDefinitions and architecture\n\nDefine the input waveform as x ∈ RC×L , with C channels of L samples at a sample rate sr. The\naudio tokenizer is a pair of encoder E and decoder D:\n \\mathcal {E}: R^{C \\times L} \\rightarrow R^{d \\times l} \\\\ \\\\ \\mathcal {D}: R^{d \\times l} \\rightarrow R^{C \\times L} \\\\ \\\\ l = \\frac {L}{f_t}; \\quad f_t = \\prod _{i} s_i\n\nwhere ft is the temporal compression factor, obtained as the product of layer strides si , and d is\nthe number of latent channels. Unlike the visual case, no spatial axis is present, so a tokenizer is\ncharacterized by the triple (sample rate, latent frame rate sr/ft , channels d). KVAE-Audio operates\nat 48 kHz with a 50 Hz latent of 64 channels, i.e. ft = 960 and one latent frame per 20 ms of audio.\nKVAE-Audio inherits its convolutional backbone from DAC [52]: residual units with dilated convolutions followed by strided convolutions that compress the signal in time and widen it in channels, with\na mirrored decoder built on transposed convolutions. We keep the Snake activation x + sin2 (αx)/α,\nwhose periodic nature is the right inductive bias for oscillatory signals and suppresses tonal artifacts.\nRelative to the original codec we introduce the following changes.\nContinuous bottleneck. The residual vector quantizer and its codebooks are replaced by a reparameterization module predicting the parameters of a Gaussian posterior and sampling a continuous vector\nfrom it. This is the change that converts the codec into a VAE, and it is what makes the model usable\nas a tokenizer for latent diffusion at all: diffusion in a continuous space does not benefit from the\ndiscrete code structure a transmission codec is optimized for.\nStride ladder and depth. One block is added to both encoder and decoder, and the stride schedule\nis re-factorized to [2, 3, 4, 5, 8] instead of the original [2, 4, 8, 8], with additional projections at the\nencoder output and the decoder input. The product 960 places the latent at exactly 50 Hz for a 48\nkHz input, against the roughly 86 Hz of the original codec. Compared to a shorter ladder with larger\nindividual strides, the additional stage buys compression without forcing single blocks to cover a\nreceptive field their residual units cannot support.\nLatent channels. The latent carries 64 channels, against 1024 in the codec it derives from and\n40–256 in the continuous tokenizers we compare against. The channel count is the sharpest trade-off\nin the whole design and is ablated in Sec. 6.7.\nAttention in the bottleneck. A single self-attention block operates on the 50 Hz sequence before the\nlatent projection. At this frame rate attention is inexpensive — one second of audio is 50 tokens —\nand it lets the latent rely on context well beyond the local convolutional window.\n6.3\n\nLatent representation alignment\n\nA recurring theme across all modalities in this report is that a tokenizer trained purely for reconstruction does not necessarily produce a latent space that is easy for a generative model to learn, and\nthat aligning representations to a frozen foundation model is one way to improve it (Sec. 5.1). For\nKVAE-Audio we regularize the encoder output towards the representation of a frozen audio foundation model, having searched over the choice of that model, the layer the target is taken from, and\nthe mechanism bringing the two representations into correspondence. The configuration we settled\non improves diffusability, and with it prompt following, without a noticeable cost in reconstruction\nquality.\n\n13\n\n\f6.4\n\nTraining methodology\n\nObjectives. Training combines a waveform-domain reconstruction term, a multi-scale spectral term,\nKL regularization of the posterior, an adversarial term, an auxiliary perceptual term computed on\nfrozen audio features, and the alignment regularizer of Sec. 6.3:\n  \\begin {split} L = \\; & \\mathcal {L}_1(x, \\hat {x}) + w_{\\text {spec}} \\cdot \\mathcal {L}_{\\text {spec}}(x, \\hat {x}) + w_{\\text {KL}} \\cdot D_{KL}(\\mathcal {E}(x), \\mathcal {N}(0, I)) \\\\ & + w_{\\text {adv}} \\cdot \\text {GAN}(\\hat {x}, x) + w_{\\text {perc}} \\cdot \\mathcal {L}_{\\text {perc}}(x, \\hat {x}) + w_{\\text {align}} \\cdot \\mathcal {L}_{\\text {align}}(\\mathcal {E}(x), \\mathcal {F}(x)) \\end {split} \nwhere x̂ = D(E(x)s ) and Lspec is a sum of L1 distances between log-magnitude mel-spectrograms\ncomputed at several window sizes [52]. In the last term F denotes a frozen audio foundation model\nproviding the target representation and Lalign the correspondence between it and the latent; the identity\nof F and the form of Lalign are deferred to the dedicated publication, as stated in Sec. 6.3. Both wperc\nand walign are set so that neither dominates the spectral term, which we found to be the fastest way to\nlose high-frequency detail: the two act on the same signal from different directions, and the useful\nrange for their sum is narrower than for either alone. All objectives are optimized by Adam [41] with\nlearning rates fixed within each stage.\nDiscriminators. Adversarial supervision is provided by an ensemble of discriminators of the kind\nestablished in the neural codec and vocoder literature — period-based discriminators operating on\nreshaped waveform segments [46] together with spectral discriminators at several STFT resolutions [52]. We compared several such configurations, varying both the composition of the ensemble\nand the resolutions it covers. The comparison confirmed the general finding of that literature: no\nsingle discriminator family is sufficient on its own, since each is sensitive to a different class of\nartifact, and combinations covering both the temporal and the spectral view behave more stably in the\nlate stages of training, when the remaining errors are concentrated in the upper band.\nPerceptual objectives. For Lperc we compared several perceptual losses computed on frozen audio\nrepresentations, differing in the backbone providing the features, the depth at which they are taken,\nand how the distance between them is formed. Perceptual terms of this kind interact strongly with the\nrest of the objective: they are effective at removing the artifacts that spectral distances tolerate, but a\nweight high enough to change the character of the reconstruction also pulls the model away from\nfaithful high-frequency detail, so the useful range is narrow and has to be found empirically for a\ngiven backbone — and, as noted above, jointly with the alignment weight rather than in isolation.\nThe configuration used for the released checkpoints follows from this comparison together with the\nalignment study of Sec. 6.3, and is left for the dedicated publication.\nCrop length schedule. Following the sequence-length scaling used for the video tokenizers (Sec. 3),\nearly stages train on 0.38-second crops and later stages increase the crop length to 5 seconds. Short\ncrops maximize the number of independent examples per batch and carry the model cheaply through\nthe bulk of convergence; long crops matter for two reasons specific to audio. They expose the model\nto slow envelopes — reverberation tails, sustained tones, musical phrase structure — that do not fit\ninto a short window at all, and they bring the training distribution closer to the segment lengths the\ntokenizer will see when serving a generative model.\nDecoder fine-tuning. The final stage freezes the encoder and fine-tunes the decoder alone. This is\nclose to a free lunch: the latent is fixed at that point, so reconstruction improves without invalidating\na generative model already trained on that latent space, and the tokenizer can keep improving after\nthe generative training has started.\nData. Training data combines open and proprietary sources spanning speech, music and general\nsound events. One filter is worth stating explicitly: a substantial fraction of nominally 48 kHz audio\nin the wild is upsampled from lower rates and carries no energy in the upper band. Training a\nfull-band tokenizer on such material teaches it to reproduce an empty spectrum, so we estimate the\ntrue bandwidth of every file and filter accordingly. After filtering, the training set amounts to roughly\n10 thousand hours.\n6.5\n\nReconstruction\n\nThere is no commonly accepted benchmark for audio tokenizers, and, unlike in the visual domain,\nthe relevant content spans domains with incompatible characteristics — a model that reconstructs\nspeech well may fail on dense polyphonic music or on broadband transients. We therefore evaluate on\nthree public test sets, one per domain: AudioSet eval [26] for general sound, MUSDB18-HQ [74] for\n14\n\n\fmusic, and EARS [75] for speech, the last being natively 48 kHz with many speakers and expressive\nstyles.\nReported metrics cover three aspects. Spectral distances — STFT and mel, each averaged over\ntwo window configurations and computed on linear and logarithmic magnitudes — measure the\nperceptually relevant error. A sample-wise waveform distance is reported alongside them because it is\nthe only one of the three that is sensitive to phase, which the spectral distances ignore by construction\nand which a waveform tokenizer is meant to preserve. Signal-level ratios (SI-SDR, SDR, SNR)\nquantify distortion energy, with SI-SDR invariant to overall level. For speech we additionally report\nPESQ-WB [37].\nIn all reconstruction experiments the encoder output is taken as the posterior mean rather than a sample\nfrom it, for our model and for every baseline alike. Sampling would add a stochastic component that\nreflects the strength of the KL regularization more than the quality of the representation, and it is the\nmean that a generative model is trained to predict.\nBaselines are open tokenizers with released weights, evaluated by us under an identical protocol:\nthe 44.1 kHz MMAudio VAE [16], chosen as the primary baseline because it is the autoencoder\nmost often found under open text-to-audio-video systems; the DAC-VAE of MovieGen Audio [69],\nchosen because, like ours, it derives from DAC; and SAME-L [68], the autoencoder of Stable Audio\n3 [24]. Since the MMAudio VAE and SAME-L operate at 44.1 kHz, all reconstructions — ours and\nMovieGen’s included — are resampled to 44.1 kHz before the metrics are computed to make the\ncomparison fair.\nTab. 3 reports the outcome. KVAE-Audio leads on spectral distances on general audio and music and\nis within noise of the best model on speech, while using 64 latent channels against 128 and 256 for the\ntwo waveform baselines, and 166.9M parameters against 852.1M for SAME-L. The MMAudio VAE,\nbeing mel-based, shows the expected signature of a model that does not preserve phase: competitive\nspectral distances alongside negative SI-SDR.\nTable 3: Reconstruction results by domain, all metrics computed at 44.1 kHz. Best in bold, second\nunderlined.\n(a) AudioSet eval\nModel", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 400, "totalLines": 1811}}}
