E MovieGen\nSAME-L\nKVAE-Audio\n\n427.6M\n107.7M\n852.1M\n166.9M\n\n40\n128\n256\n64\n\n0.636\n0.669\n0.986\n0.537\n\n1.938\n2.275\n2.726\n1.770\n\n0.106\n0.029\n0.027\n0.027\n\n-32.080\n8.384\n9.586\n9.065\n\n-2.682\n9.421\n10.347\n9.920\n\n-2.686\n9.416\n10.339\n9.933\n\n(b) MUSDB18-HQ\nModel\n\n# Params\n\nLatent dim\n\nMEL↓\n\nSTFT↓\n\nWaveform↓\n\nSI-SDR↑\n\nSDR↑\n\nSNR↑\n\nMMAudio 44.1kHz\nDACVAE MovieGen\nSAME-L\nKVAE-Audio\n\n427.6M\n107.7M\n852.1M\n166.9M\n\n40\n128\n256\n64\n\n0.681\n0.519\n0.668\n0.516\n\n1.865\n1.762\n1.786\n1.725\n\n0.114\n0.024\n0.023\n0.022\n\n-40.204\n9.688\n10.278\n10.390\n\n-3.274\n10.046\n10.648\n10.675\n\n-3.273\n10.047\n10.648\n10.677\n\n(c) EARS\n\n6.6\n\nModel\n\n# Params\n\nLatent dim\n\nMEL↓\n\nSTFT↓\n\nWaveform↓\n\nSI-SDR↑\n\nSDR↑\n\nSNR↑\n\nPESQ↑\n\nMMAudio 44.1kHz\nDACVAE MovieGen\nSAME-L\nKVAE-Audio\n\n427.6M\n107.7M\n852.1M\n166.9M\n\n40\n128\n256\n64\n\n0.616\n0.453\n0.774\n0.463\n\n1.395\n1.310\n1.575\n1.314\n\n0.030\n0.006\n0.007\n0.006\n\n-29.947\n10.264\n9.939\n9.952\n\n-2.728\n10.680\n10.374\n10.377\n\n-2.697\n10.681\n10.376\n10.384\n\n2.424\n4.246\n2.982\n4.266\n\nGeneration\n\nReconstruction metrics say little about whether a latent space is a good substrate for diffusion, and, as\nSec. 4.2 shows for the visual case, the two can point in opposite directions. To measure the property\ndirectly we use a controlled tokenizer swap: a text-to-audio generator is trained once per tokenizer,\nwith the same architecture, data and number of optimizer steps, changing only the frozen autoencoder\n15\n\n\fit operates on. The generator is the DiT backbone of Kandinsky-5 [4] — latent diffusion trained\nwith flow matching, with cross-attention to text embeddings — re-adapted to a one-dimensional\naudio latent, at 0.6B parameters. Everything downstream of the tokenizer is fixed by construction, so\ndifferences in generation quality are attributable to the latent space.\nEvaluation covers the same three domains as reconstruction, on AudioCaps [40] for general sound,\nSong Describer [60] for music, and LibriSpeech test-clean [67] for zero-shot speech synthesis. We\nreport Fréchet Audio Distance [39] on three embedding backbones — PANNs [47], PaSST [50] and\nVGGish [31] — since the metric is known to depend on the backbone and agreement across three\nof them guards against artifacts of any single one. CLAP score [107] measures prompt adherence\ndirectly; on speech we additionally report WER and CER as a measure of intelligibility. CE (Content\nEnjoyment) and PQ (Production Quality) are the two components of Audiobox Aesthetics [92], a\npredictor trained on human ratings.\nTwo caveats apply to Tab. 4 and are worth stating rather than leaving to the reader. All three FAD\nbackbones operate at 16–32 kHz internally, so these numbers are structurally insensitive to the band\nabove 16 kHz: they measure whether the latent is a good substrate for generation, not whether\nthe result is full-band. And model-based aesthetic predictors are useful as a summary of human\npreference but are not independent of the modelling choices of the systems they score. The side-byside evaluation below therefore carries the main weight of the comparison rather than supplementing\nit.\nKVAE-Audio consistently outperforms compared opensource tokenizers on AudioCaps, which is\nthe general-domain benchmark closest to the intended use of the model. On music it leads on the\naesthetic components while the mel-based MMAudio VAE retains an advantage on FAD and CLAP;\non speech it leads on FAD (PANNs), on CE and on intelligibility.\nTable 4: Generation results under the fixed-generator tokenizer swap. Best in bold, second underlined.\n(a) AudioCaps\nModel\n\n# Params\n\nLatent dim\n\nCLAP↑\n\nCE↑\n\nPQ↑\n\nFAD (PANNs)↓\n\nFAD (PaSST)↓\n\nFAD (VGGish)↓\n\nMMAudio 44.1kHz\nDACVAE MovieGen\nSAME-L\nKVAE-Audio\n\n427.6M\n107.7M\n852.1M\n166.9M\n\n40\n128\n256\n64\n\n0.336\n0.313\n0.322\n0.344\n\n3.909\n3.772\n3.588\n3.982\n\n6.192\n6.167\n5.756\n6.242\n\n17.873\n20.558\n18.446\n15.381\n\n195.910\n234.312\n240.635\n193.760\n\n1.364\n1.700\n1.325\n1.210\n\n(b) Song Describer\nModel\n\n# Params\n\nLatent dim\n\nCLAP↑\n\nCE↑\n\nPQ↑\n\nFAD (PANNs)↓\n\nFAD (PaSST)↓\n\nFAD (VGGish)↓\n\nMMAudio 44.1kHz\nDACVAE MovieGen\nSAME-L\nKVAE-Audio\n\n427.6M\n107.7M\n852.1M\n166.9M\n\n40\n128\n256\n64\n\n0.356\n0.312\n0.345\n0.339\n\n7.136\n6.953\n7.076\n7.216\n\n7.707\n7.538\n7.465\n7.929\n\n5.412\n10.194\n8.442\n7.971\n\n158.599\n214.009\n250.668\n189.427\n\n0.356\n1.046\n0.987\n0.599\n\n(c) LibriSpeech test-clean\nModel\n\n# Params\n\nLatent dim\n\nCLAP↑\n\nCE↑\n\nPQ↑\n\nFAD (PANNs)↓\n\nFAD (PaSST)↓\n\nFAD (VGGish)↓\n\nWER↓\n\nCER↓\n\nMMAudio 44.1kHz\nDACVAE MovieGen\nSAME-L\nKVAE-Audio\n\n427.6M\n107.7M\n852.1M\n166.9M\n\n40\n128\n256\n64\n\n0.368\n0.413\n0.379", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 800, "totalLines": 1811}}}
