{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-aa9523ecc8041976", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1200\t0.389\n1201\t\n1202\t5.704\n1203\t5.482\n1204\t4.617\n1205\t5.906\n1206\t\n1207\t6.629\n1208\t7.052\n1209\t5.024\n1210\t6.940\n1211\t\n1212\t8.305\n1213\t5.008\n1214\t10.257\n1215\t4.677\n1216\t\n1217\t105.931\n1218\t210.478\n1219\t301.508\n1220\t185.609\n1221\t\n1222\t2.001\n1223\t1.501\n1224\t2.721\n1225\t2.138\n1226\t\n1227\t0.257\n1228\t0.911\n1229\t0.349\n1230\t0.244\n1231\t\n1232\t0.593\n1233\t1.048\n1234\t0.629\n1235\t0.576\n1236\t\n1237\tSide-by-side evaluation. Following the protocol used for the visual tokenizers, annotators are shown\n1238\tpairs of generations produced from the same prompt by generators differing only in tokenizer, and\n1239\tmark a preference independently for three categories: prompt following, technical quality in the sense\n1240\tof freedom from noise, clipping and other artifacts, and aesthetic quality. Comparisons were run\n1241\tagainst each of the three baselines separately. Win rate is computed similar to visual tokenizers with\n1242\tformula from Sec. 4.2: pairs an annotator judged equally good pull the rate toward parity, while pairs\n1243\tjudged equally bad carry no preference signal and are dropped from the denominator. Results are\n1244\tgiven in Fig. 11 – Fig. 13. KVAE-Audio is preferred over all three baselines on all three criteria,\n1245\twith win rates from 0.54 to 0.74. The margin is narrowest against the MMAudio VAE, the strongest\n1246\tbaseline on the objective metrics of Tab. 4, and widest against DACVAE MovieGen and SAME-L,\n1247\t\n1248\t16\n1249\t\n1250\t\fboth of which win individual objective metrics but lose decisively to human judgement. That reversal\n1251\tis the reason this evaluation, rather than Tab. 4, carries the weight of the comparison.\n1252\t\n1253\tFigure 11: Side-by-side evaluation of audio generations for KVAE-Audio vs MMAudio 44.1kHz\n1254\t\n1255\tFigure 12: Side-by-side evaluation of audio generations for KVAE-Audio vs SAME-L\n1256\t\n1257\tFigure 13: Side-by-side evaluation of audio generations for KVAE-Audio vs DACVAE MovieGen\n1258\t6.7\n1259\t\n1260\tDesign choices\n1261\t\n1262\tLatent channel count. The number of latent channels is the sharpest reconstruction-versus-generation\n1263\ttrade-off we encountered. We trained variants with 16, 32, 40, 64 and 128 channels under an identical\n1264\trecipe and compared them through the tokenizer swap of Sec. 6.6. Below 64 channels reconstruction\n1265\tquality degrades visibly; above it, reconstruction keeps improving but the diffusion model trained on\n1266\tthe wider latent converges more slowly and reaches worse generation quality at a fixed step budget.\n1267\t64 channels is the point at which the two curves cross, and it is markedly narrower than the 128 and\n1268\t256 channels of the waveform baselines in Tab. 3.\n1269\tOne qualification on how far this number transfers. The crossing point is a property of the pair, not of\n1270\tthe tokenizer alone: a wider latent is harder to denoise, and a generative model with more capacity\n1271\thas more room to absorb that difficulty, so we expect the optimal channel count to grow with the\n1272\t17\n1273\t\n1274\t\fsize of the model trained on top. Our generator is 0.6B parameters (Sec. 6.6), which is modest by\n1275\tcurrent standards, and 64 channels should therefore be read as the optimum at that scale rather than\n1276\tan intrinsic property of audio latents.\n1277\tThis runs opposite to the result reported for KVAE-4x16x16 in Sec. 5.2, where raising the latent to\n1278\t64 channels improved generation as well as reconstruction. We do not read this as a contradiction\n1279\tbut as evidence that channel count is not the relevant quantity in isolation. The two tokenizers sit\n1280\tat different points on the compression-versus-information-rate curve: the video model couples its\n1281\tchannel increase to an aggressive spatio-temporal compression factor and a token budget set by patch\n1282\tsize, whereas the audio model already commits a comparatively high information rate per second\n1283\tof signal at a fixed 50 Hz frame rate, so additional channels buy reconstruction the diffusion model\n1284\tcannot exploit. The general form of the observation — consistent with the reconstruction-generation\n1285\tdilemma [113] and with the spectral account of large bottlenecks in [82] — is that channel count\n1286\tmust be tuned jointly with the compression factor and the resulting sequence length, and judged on\n1287\tgeneration rather than reconstruction. Reporting both modalities in one document makes the joint\n1288\tnature of that choice visible in a way neither result would alone.\n1289\tAttention, perceptual loss and decoder fine-tuning. The three additions to the plain convolutional\n1290\trecipe were introduced cumulatively. Tab. 5 reports the relative change each one brings over a\n1291\tbaseline trained on identical data without any of them, measured on an internal test set covering the\n1292\tsame three domains as Sec. 6.5; only relative changes are given, and the absolute numbers of Tab. 3\n1293\tremain the reproducible reference.\n1294\tAttention in the bottleneck accounts for most of the spectral improvement on its own, at negligible\n1295\tcost at a 50 Hz frame rate — one second of audio is 50 tokens. It does, however, couple the model’s\n1296\tbehaviour to the crop lengths seen in training, where a purely convolutional tokenizer extends to\n1297\tarbitrary lengths for free; in combination with the crop schedule of Sec. 6.4 and windowed inference\n1298\tthis has not been a limitation in practice, but it is the reason the decoder was left convolutional.\n1299\tAdding the perceptual loss on top makes every reconstruction metric worse, and pushes the signallevel ratios marginally below the baseline. This is the trade-off described in Sec. 6.4 appearing in the\n1300\tnumbers: a term that optimizes for perceived quality is not optimizing for sample-level fidelity, and\n1301\treconstruction metrics are the wrong instrument for judging it. The reason it stays in the recipe is that\n1302\tit improves generation quality and side-by-side preference, which Sec. 6.6 measures and this table\n1303\tdoes not.\n1304\tDecoder fine-tuning then recovers the loss and goes past it, giving the best result on four of the six\n1305\tmetrics and turning the negative signal-level ratios positive. This is the practical argument for the\n1306\tstage: it is applied after the latent is frozen, so it buys reconstruction quality back without touching\n1307\tthe latent space a generative model has already been trained on.\n1308\tTable 5: Effect of attention in the bottleneck, the perceptual loss and decoder fine-tuning, introduced\n1309\tcumulatively. The baseline is the same model without all three, trained on the same data; values are\n1310\trelative changes with respect to it, so negative is better for the distance metrics and positive is better\n1311\tfor the signal-level ratios. Measured on an internal test set. Best in bold.\n1312\tConfiguration\n1313\t\n1314\tMEL↓\n1315\t\n1316\tSTFT↓\n1317\t\n1318\tWaveform↓\n1319\t\n1320\tSI-SDR↑\n1321\t\n1322\tSDR↑\n1323\t\n1324\tSNR↑\n1325\t\n1326\t+ attention\n1327\t+ attention + perceptual loss\n1328\t+ attention + perceptual loss + decoder fine-tuning\n1329\t\n1330\t-8.5%\n1331\t-5.3%\n1332\t-9.5%\n1333\t\n1334\t-8.7%\n1335\t-6.5%\n1336\t-5.2%\n1337\t\n1338\t-3.0%\n1339\t-1.5%\n1340\t-4.4%\n1341\t\n1342\t2.6%\n1343\t-0.6%\n1344\t3.7%\n1345\t\n1346\t1.5%\n1347\t-0.3%\n1348\t3.4%\n1349\t\n1350\t1.5%\n1351\t-0.3%\n1352\t3.5%\n1353\t\n1354\t7\n1355\t\n1356\tConclusion\n1357\t\n1358\tThis report presented high-quality tokenizers for rapidly developing field of text-conditioned generation. Through series of experiments with generative models in visual and audio domains we\n1359\tdemonstrated KVAE tokenizers surpassing leading alternatives on corresponding benchmarks. Moreover, we’ve identified common patterns across modalities, exploited them in design and share our\n1360\tfindings. All models are released in opensource under MIT license, allowing everyone to use them in\n1361\ttheir research and incroporate into commercial solutions.\n1362\t\n1363\t18\n1364\t\n1365\t\f8\n1366\t\n1367\tAcknowledgments\n1368\t\n1369\tWe grateful to our colleagues at Kandinsky Lab for their constant support throughout development\n1370\tprocess, especially to Denis Dimitrov as lab director, to Dmitrii Mikhailov for his work on optimization of video tokenizers which made them practical and to Vladimir Arkhipkin for insights into flow\n1371\tmatching models and multimodal architectures.\n1372\t\n1373\tReferences\n1374\t[1] Yuang Ai, Jiaming Han, Shaobin Zhuang, Weijia Mao, Xuefeng Hu, Ziyan Yang, Zhenheng Yang, Yali Wang, Huaibo Huang, Xiangyu Yue, and Hao Chen. Bitdance: Scaling\n1375\tautoregressive generative models with binary tokens, 2026. 1\n1376\tOmniDoc-TokenBench.\n1377\thttps://github.com/alibaba/\n1378\t[2] Alibaba Group.\n1379\tOmniDoc-TokenBench, 2026. Official benchmark repository. 8\n1380\t[3] Alliance for Open Media. AOM Common Test Conditions v5.0. Input Document CWG-D103o,\n1381\tAlliance for Open Media (AOMedia), 8 2023. Codec Working Group. 6\n1382\t[4] Vladimir Arkhipkin, Vladimir Korviakov, Nikolai Gerasimenko, Denis Parkhomenko, Viacheslav Vasilev, Alexey Letunovskiy, Nikolai Vaulin, Maria Kovaleva, Ivan Kirillov, Lev\n1383\tNovitskiy, Denis Koposov, Nikita Kiselev, Alexander Varlamov, Dmitrii Mikhailov, Vladimir\n1384\tPolovnikov, Andrey Shutkin, Julia Agafonova, Ilya Vasiliev, Anastasiia Kargapoltseva, Anna\n1385\tDmitrienko, Anastasia Maltseva, Anna Averchenkova, Olga Kim, Tatiana Nikulina, and Denis\n1386\tDimitrov. Kandinsky 5.0: A family of foundation models for image and video generation,\n1387\t2026. 3, 6, 16\n1388\t[5] Shuai Bai, Keqin Chen, Xuejing Liu, Jialin Wang, 